{"parentUuid":"f962860c-d0b1-4990-a7b3-6a74911c5fcc","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"e135d9ad-cba2-4036-83a3-6f5d0216b138","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01RstNY6K7Wir36yj2eqxbmd","type":"tool_result","content":"https://github.com/mikeholownych/nexus-code/pull/756","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"09f8d6b2-6510-48d1-bc17-f89eaf88c2b5","timestamp":"2026-11-23T20:02:17.433Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"https://github.com/mikeholownych/nexus-code/pull/756","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false,"gitOperation":{"pr":{"number":756,"url":"https://github.com/mikeholownych/nexus-code/pull/756","action":"created"}}},"sandboxed":false,"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"f962860c-d0b1-4990-a7b3-6a74911c5fcc","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"session_id":"d25d0d24-8aed-4b03-a951-966dc54f0666","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/mike/nexus-code","sessionId":"43bcbb67-2cff-4fa8-a05e-f8aa598df73b","version":"2.1.329","gitBranch":"main","slug":"synthetic-moseying-seahorse","dataFlowOn":true}
{"parentUuid":"09f8d6b2-6510-48d1-bc17-f89eaf88c2b5","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-fable-5-2","id":"msg_011ChLTQXPfG6tvcZG2txmBe","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01XHntFFBgxw6L7YiTEtcPcg","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"Review VR-2 slice 1 PR","subagent_type":"reviewer","prompt":"Independent security review of a GitHub PR in the repo at ~/nexus-code: branch `v1/vr2-verify-for-clients`, head `4196a306c97152ff735498d7c6bdb5a0bcdd0c75`, base `release/v1.3.0` (find the PR with `gh pr list --head v1/vr2-verify-for-clients`; `gh pr diff <n>`; confirm the head SHA and say if it moved). Title: \"feat(verify): nexus_verify, verification as an MCP tool (VR-2, slice 1)\". The design plan is `/home/mike/nexus-wt/_plans/VR2_PLAN.md` (read sections 1, 3, 4, 5 and \"Decisions taken\").\n\nWhat it adds: `nexus serve-mcp` gets a tool `nexus_verify` that an UNTRUSTED MCP caller invokes with NO arguments to have Nexus verify the change in the served workspace; it returns a verdict and a receipt id, readable via `resources/read nexus://verify-receipt/<id>`. Files: new `cli/src/core/verify_service.ts`, `cli/src/core/verify_worker.ts`; edits to `cli/src/mcp.ts`, the `serve-mcp` command in `cli/src/index.ts`, one export in `cli/src/config.ts`; tests `cli/test/verify_service.test.ts`, `cli/test/mcp_verify.test.ts`; docs. It calls the existing verify core `verifyChange` in `cli/src/core/verify.ts` (unchanged) inside a forked worker.\n\nThe claimed properties — try to BREAK each, by running wherever practical:\n1. The caller controls nothing. Find any caller-controlled input that reaches the verification: tool `arguments` of any shape (object, array, string, null, nested, `_meta`, prototype keys like `__proto__`/`constructor`), extra members of `params` or of the JSON-RPC envelope, the `resources/read` URI (path traversal, other schemes, very long ids, ids of other receipts), MCP `initialize` params/capabilities/roots, env or cwd inherited from the client. Can any of them select a verifier, a base, a workspace/path, deliverables, a timeout, sandbox/network settings, or a claimed outcome?\n2. Only the operator's verifier runs (`--authoritative-verifier` at server start). With none configured, a repo-declared/discovered verifier must not execute (BLOCKED / VERIFIER_NOT_APPROVED, nothing run). IMPORTANT: the same server exposes `run_command`/file-write tools to the same caller. Can the caller use those tools first to change what `nexus_verify` then trusts — e.g. write a project config that names a verifier or marks one approved, write an approval digest/trust file inside the workspace or the server's HOME, edit the operator's verifier script if it lives inside the workspace, change git config/hooks/attributes/filters, move HEAD/the pinned base (`git commit --amend`, `git reset`, `git update-ref`, replace refs, grafts, a shallow file), alter `.git/info`, plant `node_modules/.bin` shims on PATH for the verifier, or environment files? The PR admits a same-user limit (documented in docs/MCP_GUIDE.md); judge whether the documentation is honest and complete, and separately whether any tamper route makes a result say VERIFIED that the *design* claims to prevent (pinned base, verification-path protection from VR-1). Distinguish \"documented limit\" from \"undocumented hole\".\n3. Pinned base: weakened tests committed after server start are judged against the pin. Try: commit weakened tests then `git update-ref` the pinned SHA's branch, rewrite history so the pinned SHA is unreachable/garbage-collected, replace objects (`git replace`), change `core.worktree`/`GIT_DIR` via repo config. What does the result say in each case — it must never be VERIFIED for a weakened test.\n4. Sandbox always on, network off; refusals in read-only mode, managed policy `require_human` for exec (see `cli/src/core/policy_runtime.ts`), no-commit workspace. Any way the caller flips these via workspace files?\n5. Single flight, watchdog timeout, cancellation (`notifications/cancelled`): worker and all verifier children gone; no orphan after server exit by stdin EOF and by SIGTERM; no scratch dirs left. Kill logic lists descendants with `ps` and signals pids: check for pid-reuse/race hazards and that it can never signal a process that is not a descendant (e.g. pid 1, the server itself, the caller).\n6. Receipts: unguessable ids, in-memory cap/eviction, cannot be created/overwritten by the caller, the SHA-256 matches the served bytes, a receipt never claims more than the result. Does a receipt leak host paths, environment, or secrets from verifier output (check redaction vs what `nexus verify` CLI prints)?\n7. Worker launch: `fork` with `execArgv` `--import tsx` in source mode and plain in dist mode; is the worker path derived only from the service module's own location (not cwd/env/PATH)? Could a workspace file (e.g. a `tsx` or `node_modules` in the served workspace, NODE_OPTIONS, a `.npmrc`) change what code the worker loads?\n8. Tests: do they fail when the property is removed? Mutate (then `git checkout`) at least: accept an argument silently; allow a discovered verifier; drop the base pin; skip the kill; and run the affected test file. Run both new test files plus `test/mcp.test.ts`, `test/verify_hostile.test.ts`, `test/verify_command.test.ts` at head, one at a time.\n9. Anything in `cli/src/mcp.ts` changes that weakens existing MCP behaviour.\n\nMandatory rules:\n- Do NOT edit ~/nexus-code or existing worktrees. `git -C ~/nexus-code fetch origin && git -C ~/nexus-code worktree add --detach ~/nexus-wt/revvr2 4196a306c97152ff735498d7c6bdb5a0bcdd0c75`; `cp -a ~/nexus-code/cli/node_modules ~/nexus-wt/revvr2/cli/`; copy `~/nexus-wt/ls1b/core/target/release/{harness-core,nexus-tui}` into `~/nexus-wt/revvr2/core/target/release/`. Remove the worktree at the end (`git -C ~/nexus-code worktree remove --force ~/nexus-wt/revvr2`). No commits, pushes or GitHub comments.\n- Every run uses a throwaway HOME (`mktemp -d`); tests: `cd cli && HOME=$(mktemp -d) node --import tsx --test test/<name>.test.ts 2>&1 | tail -20` in the foreground (the MCP test file takes about 3 minutes; allow 9). To drive the server by hand: `HOME=<tmp> node cli/node_modules/tsx/dist/cli.mjs cli/src/index.ts serve-mcp -w <throwaway git repo> [--authoritative-verifier ...]` and speak newline-delimited JSON-RPC from a script kept in a separate scratch directory; see `cli/test/mcp_verify.test.ts` for the handshake and repo setup. Never read the real ~/.nexus, /etc/nexus or ~/.config/nexus. No live inference, no network.\n- Stop every process you start by its own pid; never `pkill -f`. Hard timeout on every experiment; no unbounded background loops. Aim to finish in about 45 minutes; an experiment that fails to converge twice is \"not confirmed\".\n- Shell is zsh (use `bash -c` for loops); output passes through `rtk` (use `rtk proxy <cmd>` if it looks filtered). The runner prints `ℹ pass` / `ℹ fail`.\n\nReport under 650 words: verdict (ACCEPT / ACCEPT WITH FOLLOW-UPS / REQUEST CHANGES) at the exact SHA; findings ranked with file:line, each marked confirmed-by-running or by-reading, and classified as blocking / non-blocking / documented limit; mutation results; test counts; what you did not check."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":935,"cache_read_input_tokens":396790,"output_tokens":4998,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":935,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":4998,"cache_read_input_tokens":396790,"cache_creation_input_tokens":935,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":935},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"toolInputStreamMs":28330,"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01XHntFFBgxw6L7YiTEtcPcg":{"description":"Review VR-2 slice 1 PR","subagent_type":"reviewer","prompt":"Independent security review of a GitHub PR in the repo at ~/nexus-code: branch `v1/vr2-verify-for-clients`, head `4196a306c97152ff735498d7c6bdb5a0bcdd0c75`, base `release/v1.3.0` (find the PR with `gh pr list --head v1/vr2-verify-for-clients`; `gh pr diff <n>`; confirm the head SHA and say if it moved). Title: \"feat(verify): nexus_verify, verification as an MCP tool (VR-2, slice 1)\". The design plan is `/home/mike/nexus-wt/_plans/VR2_PLAN.md` (read sections 1, 3, 4, 5 and \"Decisions taken\").\n\nWhat it adds: `nexus serve-mcp` gets a tool `nexus_verify` that an UNTRUSTED MCP caller invokes with NO arguments to have Nexus verify the change in the served workspace; it returns a verdict and a receipt id, readable via `resources/read nexus://verify-receipt/<id>`. Files: new `cli/src/core/verify_service.ts`, `cli/src/core/verify_worker.ts`; edits to `cli/src/mcp.ts`, the `serve-mcp` command in `cli/src/index.ts`, one export in `cli/src/config.ts`; tests `cli/test/verify_service.test.ts`, `cli/test/mcp_verify.test.ts`; docs. It calls the existing verify core `verifyChange` in `cli/src/core/verify.ts` (unchanged) inside a forked worker.\n\nThe claimed properties — try to BREAK each, by running wherever practical:\n1. The caller controls nothing. Find any caller-controlled input that reaches the verification: tool `arguments` of any shape (object, array, string, null, nested, `_meta`, prototype keys like `__proto__`/`constructor`), extra members of `params` or of the JSON-RPC envelope, the `resources/read` URI (path traversal, other schemes, very long ids, ids of other receipts), MCP `initialize` params/capabilities/roots, env or cwd inherited from the client. Can any of them select a verifier, a base, a workspace/