{"parentUuid":"ea7cf260-95ad-484b-a5c4-680b29f8be3c","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-fable-5-2","id":"msg_011ChLWd9ncWfFovb6J4PFwi","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01U7SECLB65B9dY6y5KALZtj","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"Review VR-2 ACP half and follow-ups","subagent_type":"reviewer","prompt":"Second-round independent security review of GitHub PR #756 in the repo at ~/nexus-code (`gh pr view 756`), branch `v1/vr2-verify-for-clients`, head `f25030a3c0c0cc64e5abbefadad06c648a9b59c4` (confirm with `gh pr view 756 --json headRefOid`; say if it moved), base `release/v1.3.0`. A first review at `4196a306c97152ff735498d7c6bdb5a0bcdd0c75` accepted the MCP half with follow-ups (read it for context if useful: it is summarised below). Review ONLY what changed since: `git diff 4196a306..f25030a3 -- cli docs` (ignore changes that merely come from merging the release branch: compare against `git diff 4196a306..origin/release/v1.3.0` if unsure).\n\nTwo things are new:\n\nA. The ACP half of unit VR-2, written by another agent (commit `fc63f747`, \"feat(acp): expose operator-bound workspace verification\"): an ACP extension method `_nexus/verify` on `nexus acp` (the Agent Client Protocol stdio server for an UNTRUSTED client; `cli/src/acp_stdio.ts`, `cli/src/acp/{types,validate,mapping}.ts`, schema `docs/protocol/acp-adapter-v2.schema.json`, tests in `cli/test/acp_stdio.test.ts`, `acp_validate.test.ts`, evidence `docs/evidence/v1.3.0/VR2.md`). It is meant to call the same `VerifyService` (`cli/src/core/verify_service.ts`) as the MCP tool. The design plan is `/home/mike/nexus-wt/_plans/VR2_PLAN.md` section 2 (\"ACP extension\") and \"Decisions taken\". Intended properties: the method accepts only an active `sessionId` and nothing else (any other member is refused, nothing runs); only the operator's verifier (given when `nexus acp` was started) runs, a discovered verifier is never approved and the client is never prompted; base commit pinned when the session starts or loads; refused while a prompt is running or stopping on that session, and prompts are refused during a verification; refused in read-only mode and where managed policy requires a human for exec; one verification at a time; the result carries a verdict and a receipt id; the receipt is read with `_nexus/receipt/get` by explicit id and is bound to the session (another session on the same connection cannot read it); `_nexus/receipt/get` without an id still returns turn receipts only; every verdict including BLOCKED is a result, an error only when no verdict exists.\n   Try to BREAK it, by running where practical (fake-sidecar tests in the style of `cli/test/acp_stdio.test.ts` / `cli/test/acp_review_fixes.test.ts`, or the real adapter over stdio):\n   1. Any client-controlled input reaching the verification: extra params, `_meta`, a `cwd`, a verifier, a base, a path, a claimed verdict; `session/new`/`session/load` `cwd` choosing WHICH directory is verified (the client legitimately picks the session cwd: is the verified tree exactly that session's workspace, and is the operator's verifier/deliverables still what is used? can a client point a session at a directory where the operator's verifier command means something else or where deliverables resolve differently, and get VERIFIED for something misleading? what does the receipt say about which workspace and base it covers?).\n   2. Base pinning per session: when exactly is it pinned (new vs load)? Can the client commit weakened tests, then open a NEW session (new pin) and get VERIFIED? If yes, is that documented as the boundary (the MCP half documents \"a commit made before the server starts becomes the base\")? Judge whether, for ACP, re-pinning on each new session makes the pin nearly meaningless against the same client, and whether docs/evidence say so honestly.\n   3. Interleavings: `_nexus/verify` during a prompt, a prompt during verify, `session/cancel` and `$/cancel_request` of the verify request (worker killed? reply? state left usable?), two verifies on two sessions, verify then connection close / SIGTERM (worker and verifier children gone, scratch removed — the adapter's signal handling was added in earlier PRs: check it also stops the verify service), the `stopping` flag interaction.\n   4. Receipts: cross-session read, forged/unknown ids, id-less `_nexus/receipt/get` unchanged, receipt content vs result, eviction.\n   5. Does `nexus acp` start-up still refuse auto approval, and does read-only / `REDACTED` refuse `_nexus/verify`?\n   6. Schema/versioning: `acp-adapter-v2.schema.json` and `ACP_ADAPTER_VERSIONS.json` — is v1 left untouched (frozen), does `initialize` advertise the extension truthfully, do the schema tests regenerate/compare?\n   7. Does the evidence doc `docs/evidence/v1.3.0/VR2.md` claim only what tests show?\n   8. Mutations (then `git checkout`): accept an extra param; allow verify during a prompt; drop the session binding of receipts; and run the relevant test files — do tests fail?\n\nB. Follow-ups to the first review of the MCP half (commit titled \"fix(verify): stop the verifier when serve-mcp is signalled; honour read-only from the environment\"): SIGTERM/SIGINT handlers in `McpServer.startStdio` (`cli/src/mcp.ts` ~969-1011) that await a new `VerifyService.stop()` and exit 143/130 with a non-unref'd hard-exit timer; a `.catch` on the un-awaited verify call; `readOnly` for the tool derived from `buildConfig` in the `serve-mcp` action of `cli/src/index.ts` (~1213-1222; note the stated caveat: if `buildConfig` throws, the server still starts and `nexus_verify` is refused as read-only); docs. Check: the handlers cannot break normal stdin-EOF shutdown or leave the process hanging; the kill ordering in `verify_service.ts` (~185: verifier SIGKILLed first, worker later) cannot signal a non-descendant; the buildConfig-throws path fails closed; the new SIGTERM test is sound.\n\nRun at head, one file at a time: `acp_stdio`, `acp_validate`, `acp_schema`, `acp_mapping`, `acp_review_fixes`, `acp_client_conformance`, `verify_service`, `mcp_verify` (about 3 minutes; allow 9), `mcp`.\n\nMandatory rules:\n- Do NOT edit ~/nexus-code or existing worktrees. `git -C ~/nexus-code fetch origin && git -C ~/nexus-code worktree add --detach ~/nexus-wt/revvr2b f25030a3c0c0cc64e5abbefadad06c648a9b59c4`; `cp -a ~/nexus-code/cli/node_modules ~/nexus-wt/revvr2b/cli/`; copy `~/nexus-wt/ls1b/core/target/release/{harness-core,nexus-tui}` into `~/nexus-wt/revvr2b/core/target/release/`. Remove the worktree at the end (`git -C ~/nexus-code worktree remove --force ~/nexus-wt/revvr2b`). No commits, pushes or GitHub comments.\n- Every run uses a throwaway HOME (`mktemp -d`): `cd cli && HOME=$(mktemp -d) node --import tsx --test test/<name>.test.ts 2>&1 | tail -20`, foreground. Never read the real ~/.nexus, /etc/nexus or ~/.config/nexus. No live inference, no network.\n- Stop every process you start by its own pid; never `pkill -f`. Hard timeout on every experiment; no unbounded background loops. Aim for about 45 minutes; an experiment that fails to converge twice is \"not confirmed\".\n- Shell is zsh (use `bash -c` for loops); output passes through `rtk` (use `rtk proxy <cmd>` if it looks filtered). The runner prints `ℹ pass` / `ℹ fail`.\n\nReport under 650 words: verdict (ACCEPT / ACCEPT WITH FOLLOW-UPS / REQUEST CHANGES) at the exact SHA; findings ranked with file:line, each marked confirmed-by-running or by-reading, classified blocking / non-blocking / documented limit, and attributed to part A or B; mutation results; test counts; what you did not check."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":675,"cache_read_input_tokens":433640,"output_tokens":3045,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":675,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":3045,"cache_read_input_tokens":433640,"cache_creation_input_tokens":675,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":675},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"toolInputStreamMs":29082,"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01U7SECLB65B9dY6y5KALZtj":{"description":"Review VR-2 ACP half and follow-ups","subagent_type":"reviewer","prompt":"Second-round independent security review of GitHub PR #756 in the repo at ~/nexus-code (`gh pr view 756`), branch `v1/vr2-verify-for-clients`, head `f25030a3c0c0cc64e5abbefadad06c648a9b59c4` (confirm with `gh pr view 756 --json headRefOid`; say if it moved), base `release/v1.3.0`. A first review at `4196a306c97152ff735498d7c6bdb5a0bcdd0c75` accepted the MCP half with follow-ups (read it for context if useful: it is summarised below). Review ONLY what changed since: `git diff 4196a306..f25030a3 -- cli docs` (ignore changes that merely come from merging the release branch: compare against `git diff 4196a306..origin/release/v1.3.0` if unsure).\n\nTwo things are new:\n\nA. The ACP half of unit VR-2, written by another agent (commit `fc63f747`, \"feat(acp): expose operator-bound workspace verification\"): an ACP extension method `_nexus/verify` on `nexus acp` (the Agent Client Protocol stdio server for an UNTRUSTED client; `cli/src/acp_stdio.ts`, `cli/src/acp/{types,validate,mapping}.ts`, schema `docs/protocol/acp-adapter-v2.schema.json`, tests in `cli/test/acp_stdio.test.ts`, `acp_validate.test.ts`, evidence `docs/evidence/v1.3.0/VR2.md`). It is meant to call the same `VerifyService` (`cli/src/core/verify_service.ts`) as the MCP tool. The design plan is `/home/mike/nexus-wt/_plans/VR2_PLAN.md` section 2 (\"ACP extension\") and \"Decisions taken\". Intended properties: the method accepts only an active `sessionId` and nothing else (any other member is refused, nothing runs); only the operator's verifier (given when `nexus acp` was started) runs, a discovered verifier is never approved and the client is never prompted; base commit pinned when the session starts or loads; refused while a prompt is running or stopping on that session, and prompts are refused during a verification; refused in read-only mode and where managed policy requires a human for exec; one verification at a time; the result carries a verdict and a receipt id; the receipt is read with `_nexus/receipt/get` by explicit id and is bound to the session (another session on the same connection cannot read it); `_nexus/receipt/get` without an id still returns turn receipts only; every verdict including BLOCKED is a result, an error only when no verdict exists.\n   Try to BREAK it, by running where practical (fake-sidecar tests in the style of `cli/test/acp_stdio.test.ts` / `cli/test/acp_review_fixes.test.ts`, or the real adapter over stdio):\n   1. Any client-controlled input reaching the verification: extra params, `_meta`, a `cwd`, a verifier, a base, a path, a claimed verdict; `session/new`/`session/load` `cwd` choosing WHICH directory is verified (the client legitimately picks the session cwd: is the verified tree exactly that session's workspace, and is the operator's verifier/deliverables still what is used? can a client point a session at a directory where the operator's verifier command means something else or where deliverables resolve differently, and get VERIFIED for something misleading? what does the receipt say about which workspace and base it covers?).\n   2. Base pinning per session: when exactly is it pinned (new vs load)? Can the client commit weakened tests, then open a NEW session (new pin) and get VERIFIED? If yes, is that documented as the boundary (the MCP half documents \"a commit made before the server starts becomes the base\")? Judge whether, for ACP, re-pinning on each new session makes the pin nearly meaningless against the same client, and whether docs/evidence say so honestly.\n   3. Interleavings: `_nexus/verify` during a prompt, a prompt during verify, `session/cancel` and `$/cancel_request` of the verify request (worker killed? reply? state left usable?), two verifies on two sessions, verify then connection close / SIGTERM (worker and verifier children gone, scratch removed — the adapter's signal handling was added in earlier PRs: check it also stops the verify service), the `stopping` flag interaction.\n   4. Receipts: cross-session read, forged/unknown ids, id-less `_nexus/receipt/get` unchanged, receipt content vs result, eviction.\n   5. Does `nexus acp` start-up still refuse auto approval, and does read-only / `REDACTED` refuse `_nexus/verify`?\n   6. Schema/versioning: `acp-adapter-v2.schema.json` and `ACP_ADAPTER_VERSIONS.json` — is v1 left untouched (frozen), does `initialize` advertise the extension truthfully, do the schema tests regenerate/compare?\n   7. Does the evidence doc `docs/evidence/v1.3.0/VR2.md` claim only what tests show?\n   8. Mutations (then `git checkout`): accept an extra param; allow verify during a prompt; drop the session binding of receipts; and run the relevant test files — do tests fail?\n\nB. Follow-ups to the first review of the MCP half (commit titled \"fix(verify): stop the verifier when serve-mcp is signalled; honour read-only from the environment\"): SIGTERM/SIGINT handlers in `McpServer.startStdio` (`cli/src/mcp.ts` ~969-1011) that await a new `VerifyService.stop()` and exit 143/130 with a non-unref'd hard-exit timer; a `.catch` on the un-awaited verify call; `readOnly` for the tool derived from `buildConfig` in the `serve-mcp` action of `cli/src/index.ts` (~1213-1222; note the stated caveat: if `buildConfig` throws, the server still starts and `nexus_verify` is refused as read-only); docs. Check: the handlers cannot break normal stdin-EOF shutdown or leave the process hanging; the kill ordering in `verify_service.ts` (~185: verifier SIGKILLed first, worker later) cannot signal a non-descendant; the buildConfig-throws path fails closed; the new SIGTERM test is sound.\n\nRun at head, one file at a time: `acp_stdio`, `acp_validate`, `acp_schema`, `acp_mapping`, `acp_review_fixes`, `acp_client_conformance`, `verify_service`, `mcp_verify` (about 3 minutes; allow 9), `mcp`.\n\nMandatory rules:\n- Do NOT edit ~/nexus-code or existing worktrees. `git -C ~/nexus-code fetch origin && git -C ~/nexus-code worktree add --detach ~/nexus-wt/revvr2b f25030a3c0c0cc64e5abbefadad06c648a9b59c4`; `cp -a ~/nexus-code/cli/node_modules ~/nexus-wt/revvr2b/cli/`; copy `~/nexus-wt/ls1b/core/target/release/{harness-core,nexus-tui}` into `~/nexus-wt/revvr2b/core/target/release/`. Remove the worktree at the end (`git -C ~/nexus-code worktree remove --force ~/nexus-wt/revvr2b`). No commits, pushes or GitHub comments.\n- Every run uses a throwaway HOME (`mktemp -d`): `cd cli && HOME=$(mktemp -d) node --import tsx --test test/<name>.test.ts 2>&1 | tail -20`, foreground. Never read the real ~/.nexus, /etc/nexus or ~/.config/nexus. No live inference, no network.\n- Stop every process you start by its own pid; never `pkill -f`. Hard timeout on every experiment; no unbounded background loops. Aim for about 45 minutes; an experiment that fails to converge twice is \"not confirmed\".\n- Shell is zsh (use `bash -c` for loops); output passes through `rtk` (use `rtk proxy <cmd>` if it looks filtered). The runner prints `ℹ pass` / `ℹ fail`.\n\nReport under 650 words: verdict (ACCEPT / ACCEPT WITH FOLLOW-UPS / REQUEST CHANGES) at the exact SHA; findings ranked with file:line, each marked confirmed-by-running or by-reading, classified blocking / non-blocking / documented limit, and attributed to part A or B; mutation results; test counts; what you did not check."}},"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011ChLWd9bSVxtcRibqWtAi2","requestRef":"bdeb8145