 shape gets a different document. Fix: keep the id-less path on turn receipts only, with a separate map for verification receipts, and update the test.\n\n2. **Part A, documented limit not yet documented (cwd/workspace choice):** a verification covers whatever directory the client passes as `session/new` or `session/load` `cwd`.\n   - `session.cwd` is used as the worktree, and the base is pinned from `HEAD` of that directory.\n   - The operator's verifier command and deliverable list are fixed at startup, but deliverables are normalised against the launch workspace (`config.ts:529`) and then resolved by the worker against the session cwd.\n   - So a client-chosen repo containing, for example, a `package.json` whose test is `exit 0` can get a verdict.\n   - The receipt does record `workspace` and `base_pinned_at`. The `_nexus/verify` summary omits `workspace`, so a client reading only the result cannot see which tree was covered.\n   - `docs/evidence/v1.3.0/VR2.md` does not mention any of this.\n   - Fix: document it, add `workspace` to the summary, and either refuse a `cwd` outside the operator's `--workspace` or state that the client chooses the tree.\n\n3. **Part A, documented limit not yet documented (base re-pinning):** the base is pinned in `startSession` for both new and load (`acp_stdio.ts:~229`). A client can commit weakened tests, open a new session, and get a base that already contains the weakening. The only protection is that the client cannot move the base of an existing session.\n   - The client also approves tool calls itself in review mode, so it can make those commits.\n   - The evidence doc says \"before an ACP session starts is the baseline\". That is true but does not say each new session re-pins.\n   - For ACP, the pin only protects a single session. Say so, or have the operator pin once at `nexus acp` start.\n\n4. **Part A, test gap, confirmed by running (mutation 2 below):** nothing tests refusal of verify during a prompt or a stopping turn, or of a prompt during a verify. There is also no read-only refusal test for ACP. The cross-session receipt test at `acp_stdio.test.ts:~125` uses a session id that is not active, so it proves little. Fix: add tests for verify-during-prompt, prompt-during-verify and read-only, and use a second real session for the cross-session receipt test.\n\n5. **Part A, non-blocking:** shutdown timing. The ACP signal deadline is 10 s (`ACP_SHUTDOWN_GRACE_MS`), the same as `VERIFY_KILL_GRACE_MS`. A verifier that ignores SIGTERM is killed at 10 s, but `process.exit` can fire first, and the exit hook then SIGKILLs the worker, so scratch trees may be left behind. MCP avoids this with a 15 s hard exit. Fix: make the ACP deadline at least `VERIFY_KILL_GRACE_MS + 5s` when a verification is running. Not run; by reading only.\n\n6. **Part A, minor:** `_meta` is accepted on `_nexus/verify` while the property says nothing else is accepted. It is never read, so it reaches no execution path. Say so in the docs, or reject it.\n\n**What held up**\n- Extra, forged or claimed members (`verifier`, `base`, `cwd`, verdict) are refused before anything runs; this is tested.\n- Verify and `startingSessions`/overlap checks are synchronous, so two verifies cannot race.\n- `$/cancel_request` aborts the worker and replies once; this is tested.\n- `close()` aborts and awaits the verification, and SIGINT/SIGTERM and stdin end both go through `close()`.\n- Read-only comes from the same resolved config, and the auto-approval startup refusal is unchanged.\n- `acp-adapter-v1.schema.json` is untouched. The v2 ledger entry exists. `gen_acp_schema.mjs --check` reports v2 current.\n- `initialize` advertises the extension truthfully.\n- Receipt lookup is bound to the session in `VerifyService`.\n\n**Part B**\n- The MCP signal handlers are sound. They are registered with `process.on` and do not keep the loop alive. The hard-exit timer is only armed after a signal, and the `stopping` guard makes repeat signals harmless. stdin-EOF shutdown is unchanged. Exit codes 143/130 are correct.\n- The 15 s hard exit gives the worker 5 s after the verifier is killed at 10 s, so scratch removal is plausible.\n- The SIGKILL-without-worker step uses a fresh `descendants()` listing and the timers are cleared on settle. A signalled non-descendant would need a pid-reuse race inside that window. Negligible.\n- `buildConfig` fails closed: `readOnly` defaults to `true`, and the catch path logs a diagnostic.\n- The new SIGTERM test is sound: it checks exit 143, worker and verifier gone, and no verdict. I did not run it against the pre-fix code.\n\n**Mutations (each run, then `git checkout`)**\n- Accept an extra param: `acp_stdio` 8/9 pass and `acp_validate` 14/15 pass, so the tests fail as they should.\n- Allow verify during a prompt: `acp_stdio` 9/9 and `acp_review_fixes` 8/8 still pass, so this mutation is not caught (finding 4).\n- Drop the receipt session binding: `acp_stdio` 8/9 and `verify_service` 9/10 pass, so the tests fail as they should.\n\n**Test counts at head** (throwaway HOME, one file at a time, 0 failures in every file): `acp_stdio` 9, `acp_validate` 15, `acp_schema` 7, `acp_mapping` 22, `acp_review_fixes` 8, `acp_client_conformance` 2, `verify_service` 10, `mcp_verify` 7, `mcp` 4.\n\n**Evidence doc:** the claims match the tests except for the omissions in findings 2 and 3. It claims \"69 passed\" from a run at a different base, which I did not reproduce. The doc says the run was on `4196a306` plus a patch, and \"No hosted CI has run\".\n\n**Not checked:** a real-adapter stdio run with a real worker (I used mutation runs and reading), SIGTERM of `nexus acp` during a live verify, two verifies on two sessions, receipt eviction under ACP, Node 22.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>67484</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>15</tool_uses><duration_ms>394511</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>\n</system-reminder>"}]}}
