{"type":"api-request-blob","sessionId":"43bcbb67-2cff-4fa8-a05e-f8aa598df73b","hash":"d5896844e10241dc52e8a5cd804eed1069f1a13af0738a4fa1bc6b37be9c35fe","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The fix is committed and pushed, and all five audit test files pass with it. Opening it as a PR into #771's branch so its author can take it without me touching their branch."},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01XgcNAN7LdpjGLzESZ7PuFa","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/tmp/claude-1000/-home-mike-nexus-code/43bcbb67-2cff-4fa8-a05e-f8aa598df73b/scratchpad/pr_cgit.md","content":"A fix for the blocker in the review of #771, as a PR **into the #771 branch** so its author can take it or leave it. It is based on `403f197d`.\n\n## The problem\n\nThe candidate binding makes Git read the workspace named by recorded state. A content filter or a file-system monitor that the repository defines for itself then runs, with the rights of whoever runs `nexus audit export`. In review, nine configurations executed a planted command (clean and process filters, `core.fsmonitor`, `include.path`, `includeIf`, `core.attributesFile`, `.git/info/attributes`, a `.git` file pointing at another git directory, `core.worktree`). An audit export is the command one runs on a workspace one does not trust.\n\n## The fix\n\nBefore the candidate identity is computed, `audit_export.ts` lists the repository's own configuration, including what it includes, for keys that name a program Git runs or that redirect where Git looks: `filter.*`, `core.fsmonitor`, `core.attributesFile`, `core.worktree`, `core.hooksPath`, `core.sshCommand`, `core.gitProxy`, `diff.*.textconv`, `diff.*.command`, `diff.external`. Any such key outside the operator's system, global or command-line scope makes the binding `unavailable` with reason `workspace_configures_git_programs`. A listing that cannot be read counts as a hit. Reading configuration runs none of these programs.\n\nNothing in the shared routine (`candidate.ts`) changes, so receipt digests are exactly what they were.\n\n## Trade-off\n\nA repository that legitimately defines a filter in its own `.git/config` (for example `git lfs install --local`) now gets `unavailable` from the binding instead of a comparison. That is the conservative answer and it is visible in the report. A filter configured at the operator's global level is still honoured, because that configuration is the operator's to trust.\n\n## Tests\n\nNew test in `audit_export.test.ts` with five planted configurations (a clean filter; a filter named only in `.git/info/attributes`; a filter defined in an included file; a file-system monitor; an attributes file named by the repository). For each: the planted command's marker file must not exist after the export, and the binding must be `unavailable` with the new reason. With the check disabled the test fails.\n\nLocally on Node 24: `audit_export` 20/20, `audit_command` 8/8, `audit_bundle` 25/25, `audit_schema` 7/7, `leak_guard` 6/6, `tsc --noEmit` clean.\n\n## Not done here\n\n- The same exposure exists for every other caller of the candidate-identity routine (`agent.ts`, `spine_handlers.ts`, `worktree.ts`, `state.ts`, `operation_runner_execution.ts`). Those run in the operator's own session on the operator's workspace, which is a different trust situation; it deserves its own decision.\n- Not covered by the new test: `includeIf`, a `.git` file pointing elsewhere, `core.worktree`, a process filter, a registered submodule. The key list covers the first four by reading; a submodule's own configuration is not listed by this check.\n- The other open items from the review of #771 (the `no_receipts` warning, `docs/AUDIT.md`, the worktree path question, tests for the scan bounds).\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01GzNuUK1dgFW67wnnS6U8Pm","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd ~/nexus-code && b=$(gh pr view 771 --json headRefName --jq .headRefName) && u=$(gh pr create --base \"$b\" --head v1/audit-binding-refuse-repo-programs --title \"fix(audit): do not hash a repository whose own Git configuration names a program\" --body-file /tmp/claude-1000/-home-mike-nexus-code/43bcbb67-2cff-4fa8-a05e-f8aa598df73b/scratchpad/pr_cgit.md | tail -1); echo \"$u\"; n=${u##*/}; gh pr comment 771 --body \"The blocker has a fix ready as a PR into this branch: #$n (\\\"do not hash a repository whose own Git configuration names a program\\\"). It adds a check in \\`audit_export.ts\\` before the candidate identity is computed and a test with five planted configurations; it does not touch \\`candidate.ts\\`, so receipt digests are unchanged. Merge it here, or reimplement it differently, and I will re-review. The newest commit here (\\`403f197d\\`) still changes test expectations only.\" | tail -1; echo \"$(date -u +%FT%TZ) PR (claude-invariants-audit, reviewer): $u, a fix for the #771 blocker, opened INTO Codex's branch $b (not pushed to it), from ~/nexus-wt/cgit. audit export refuses (binding 'unavailable', reason workspace_configures_git_programs) a repository whose own git config names filters/fsmonitor/etc.; candidate.ts untouched. #771 itself is still REQUEST CHANGES at 403f197d (test-only commits since the review). DO NOT MERGE #771 without this or an equivalent.\" >> \"$(git rev-parse --git-common-dir)/nexus-claims/nexus-claims.log\"; gh pr view 771 --json state,headRefOid --jq '[.state,.headRefOid[0:8]]|@tsv'","description":"Open the fix as a PR into PR 771's branch, point to it from 771, log it, and re-read 771's state"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}]}}
