{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-11-24T09:31:50.503Z","sessionId":"43bcbb67-2cff-4fa8-a05e-f8aa598df73b","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>b524zndai</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>toolu_01Gr1uYQymidbwvoXQiU93UY</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>/tmp/claude-1000/-home-mike-nexus-code/d25d0d24-8aed-4b03-a951-966dc54f0666/tasks/b524zndai.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Background command \"cd /home/mike/nexus-wt/rev774/cli &amp;&amp; bash -c 'for t in p10_task_elapsed_budget leak_guard protocol_parity protocol_schema run_json_contract check_requirements p10_inference_submission_budget turn_deadline_abort; do echo \"== $t\"; HOME=$(mktemp -d) timeout 540 node --import tsx --test test/$t.test.ts 2&gt;&amp;1 | grep -E \"^ℹ (pass|fail)|not ok\" | head -8; done; npx tsc --noEmit -p . 2&gt;&amp;1 | tail -5; echo tsc=$?'\" completed (exit code 0)</summary>\n</task-notification>"}
{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-11-24T09:31:51.793Z","sessionId":"43bcbb67-2cff-4fa8-a05e-f8aa598df73b","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>ac19cad41192e3f0a</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>toolu_01WxAHM337b5m8R97TLcS9CA</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>/tmp/claude-1000/-home-mike-nexus-code/d25d0d24-8aed-4b03-a951-966dc54f0666/tasks/ac19cad41192e3f0a.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Plan BG-1, BG-2 and HK units\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>I've written both plans:\n- `/home/mike/nexus-wt/_plans/BG_PLAN.md`\n- `/home/mike/nexus-wt/_plans/HK_PLAN.md`\n\nBoth are read-only against `origin/release/v1.3.0` at `4ae25a10`, with every claim cited by file:line. No code was edited.\n\n## Slices (hot files in bold)\n**BG-1**\n1. Library (M): process identity, strict JSON reader, runner store, unattended policy, job receipt schema and ledger. No hot files.\n2. Runner, worker, `runner serve`, `job start|status|stop` (L): also adds the kernel start time to the operation lock and an `origin` field to the operation record (operation schema v5). **`index.ts`**, one register line.\n3. Operator surface (M): `job list|logs|resume`, `runner status|install-unit`, doctor findings, docs and examples. **`doctor.ts`**.\n\n**BG-2**\n1. Schedule library (M): UTC cron, `every` and `once` triggers; schema and ledger.\n2. Runner integration plus the three acceptance tests and the hostile-schedule tests (M). No hot files.\n3. Unattended stamp on tool receipts (S): one plain property, no new branch. **`dispatch_tool_call.ts`**, **`doctor.ts`**.\n\n**HK-1**\n1. Library (M): hook runner, content-hash trust store, hooks schema.\n2. Spine wiring (M): pre-tool attaches to the spine's unused `contextAdmission` stage, post-tool to `evidenceEmission`. Touches `agent.ts`, which another agent has claimed. No edits to `dispatchToolCallImpl`, `run_turn.ts` or `sidecar.ts`.\n3. `nexus hooks` commands, docs and examples (S). **`index.ts`**, **`doctor.ts`**.\n\n**HK-2**\n1. Manifest, commit-pinned install, `install|list|remove|verify`, policy check at install (M). **`index.ts`**.\n2. Load-time verification, plugin hooks, load errors reported instead of swallowed, docs fix (M). **`doctor.ts`**.\n3. Trigger-loaded skills, MCP server contributions, request-shape test (M).\n\n**HK-3**\n1. Per-file approval for workspace JS plugins, migration and deprecation note (M). **`doctor.ts`**.\n2. Workspace manifest plugins whose hooks run as subprocesses (S/M, may roll to v1.4).\n\n## Decisions taken\n- **D15:** `nexus runner serve` runs in the foreground under a documented `systemd --user` unit, with one worker process per job. Rejected alternatives: re-parenting the sidecar, double-fork, `systemd-run` per job.\n- **Liveness:** each worker holds a kernel lock (flock). Signals go only to a pid whose kernel start time and boot id still match.\n- **Attach:** the client talks to the runner through inbox files; you follow a job through its log and state only. Live attach is left to PF-3.\n- **D19:** jobs are `read-only` by default; `--allow-worktree-write` is explicit. Unattended runs use a policy that allows only writes in the fresh worktree; exec and egress are denied. Never auto mode, `--yes` or full access. Jobs are always git-isolated at a base commit frozen when submitted.\n- **Crash handling:** after a worker crash the job resumes automatically, at most 3 times, through the existing SI-7 recovery.\n- **D21:** event triggers and SSH stay in v1.4. Also deferred: wake checks, time zones, schedule spec files. Missed runs are reported, not run, by default.\n- **Hooks:** two events only, pre-tool and post-tool. Allow and `updatedInput` are ignored, `ask` counts as deny, and pre-tool fails closed. An invalid operator hooks file denies every call. Project hooks run only after their exact file hash is approved.\n- **Plugins:** contribute hooks, trigger-loaded skills and MCP servers only — no in-process code, custom tools or verifiers. Git installs need a full commit id, and Nexus computes its own digest of the plugin's files.\n- **D18:** in-process JS plugins stay, but workspace ones need per-file approval.\n\n## Main risks\n- `UnattendedApprovalPolicy` duplicates approval logic. It has an exhaustive table test, but must track any later change to `ApprovalPolicy`.\n- Worktree-write jobs can only run commands you allowed in `command_rules.json`.\n- Under systemd, a runner crash also ends its workers, so a crash mid-tool always needs a human decision.\n- Hooks run after the interactive approval prompt.\n- `agent.ts`, `index.ts`, `doctor.ts`, `plugins.ts` and `mcp.ts` are being edited by other agents.\n- Edits by programs running as the same user are not detected.\n\n## Could not determine\n- Whether command `allow` rules match compound commands segment by segment, and how bwrap isolates pids. Both affect how the SI-7-against-runner test is written.\n- Whether `nexus audit export` covers operations.\n- Whether operation units can answer `NEEDS_INPUT` questions.\n- GV-5's final plugin and MCP API. I read it from an uncommitted worktree.\n- Whether the TUI can ask about project-hook approval without a protocol change.\n- Whether git hosts allow fetching an unadvertised commit by id at depth 1. The plan has a fallback.\n\nXERJ has only the opencode corpus loaded, so the goose, Gemini CLI and Codex parts rely on the plan's own peer notes.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>328813</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>107</tool_uses><duration_ms>2007105</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
