{"type":"api-request-blob","sessionId":"43bcbb67-2cff-4fa8-a05e-f8aa598df73b","hash":"238d6980e707e859a41d102e721b2312c3001ce9a1f5bb92586c40afac78060e","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Xaig159abwHVpxpMEJtkJr","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"Implement BG-1 slices 1-2","subagent_type":"general-purpose","prompt":"Implement unit BG-1 (detached, resumable background runs) of the Nexus Code v1.3.0 plan, following the written design exactly: `/home/mike/nexus-wt/_plans/BG_PLAN.md`. Read the whole plan first (sections 0, 1, 2, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8 and \"Decisions taken\" are your spec for BG-1; section 3 is BG-2 and is NOT your job, but the pure modules BG-2 needs that the plan assigns to BG-1 slice 1, such as the unattended policy, are). Work ONLY in the git worktree `/home/mike/nexus-wt/bg1` (branch `v1/bg1-runner-lib`, cut from `origin/release/v1.3.0`). Do not push, open PRs, or touch any other worktree or `~/nexus-code` itself. Do not ask questions; where the plan leaves a detail open choose the conservative option and list it in your report.\n\nThe unit's acceptance, from `docs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md`: \"kill the CLI; the run continues and resumes\". Gate G-4 for unattended runs: no allow-always, fresh worktree, kill-and-resume.\n\nDeliver the plan's slices in order, COMMITTING LOCALLY at the end of each slice (one commit per slice, message `feat(background): <what> (BG-1, slice N)` ending with the line `Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.2 <noreply@anthropic.com>`), so that each slice is independently green:\n- Slice 1 (library, no hot files): process identity (pid + kernel start time + boot id), the strict JSON reader, the runner store and job record `nexus-job` v1 with its schema and ledger entry, the unattended approval policy as a pure module with its exhaustive table test, the job receipt schema and ledger. Tests as the plan names them.\n- Slice 2 (runner, worker, `runner serve`, `job start|status|stop`): as the plan specifies, including the kernel start time on the operation lock and the `origin` field (operation schema bump per the repo's schema rules), and the acceptance test: start a job, kill the client CLI, the run continues; kill the worker, it resumes (bounded retries); a job runs in a fresh worktree; an action that would prompt is denied, never allowed-always.\n- Slice 3 (operator surface: `job list|logs|resume`, `runner status|install-unit`, doctor findings, docs and a runnable example for gate G-6) ONLY if slices 1 and 2 are complete, committed and green. If you run short, stop cleanly after a finished slice and say so; never leave a half-wired command.\n\nHard constraints:\n- No surface here may weaken an approval gate, verification-path protection, managed policy (`cli/src/core/policy_runtime.ts`: `approval.strict`, `disable_auto`, …) or the VERIFIED outcome ladder. An unattended job never runs in auto mode, never grants allow-always, and refuses `--yes`.\n- A CI gate fails if `runTurnImpl`, `dispatchToolCallImpl`, `runSubagent`, `compactNowImpl` or `driveOperation` grow in cyclomatic complexity or nesting (`cli/critical-complexity-baseline.json`). Do not add branches there; run the gate from the worktree root: `node cli/node_modules/tsx/dist/cli.mjs cli/scripts/check_critical_complexity.ts`.\n- Do not add or lengthen model-facing tool descriptions (`cli/test/context_convergence.test.ts` pins a size budget).\n- Other agents hold file claims on `cli/src/index.ts`, `cli/src/doctor.ts`, `cli/src/config.ts`, `cli/src/types.ts`. You may edit them only with small self-contained additions (one register call for the new commands; new doctor findings in their own function); put the command implementations in new files as the plan says. Do not reformat or move existing code.\n- Process handling: signal a process only after checking pid + kernel start time (+ boot id) still match; never `pkill`; every runner/worker/tool process a test starts is stopped by its pid in a `finally`. State directories and files are created 0700/0600 and tests assert it.\n- Every new on-disk format or protocol message gets a JSON schema and a version ledger entry where the repo keeps them (`docs/protocol/`, see how `VERSIONS.json`, `RUN_RESULT_VERSIONS.json`, `AUDIT_*` ledgers and their tests work), and earlier frozen schema versions stay byte-identical. If you change the sidecar protocol schema, regenerate `cli/test/fixtures/protocol_v1_schema_samples.jsonl` with `node cli/scripts/gen_protocol_samples.mjs` (CI checks it).\n- Linux x86_64 only. Code must work on Node 22 and 24 (only 24 is installed here: avoid Node-24-only APIs).\n\nWorking rules (mandatory):\n- Tests: `cd /home/mike/nexus-wt/bg1/cli && HOME=$(mktemp -d) node --import tsx --test test/<name>.test.ts 2>&1 | tail -30` (the runner prints `ℹ pass` / `ℹ fail`), one file at a time, foreground. A throwaway HOME (and `NEXUS_STATE_DIR` where the plan says) is mandatory: never read or write the real `~/.nexus`, `/etc/nexus`, `~/.config/nexus`. No live inference (use the scripted gateway `cli/test/helpers/fixtures.ts` `startGateway`), no network.\n- New test files import `./helpers/leak_guard.js` first (the repo's `leak_guard.test.ts` fails a file that builds an agent, bridge or gateway without it); clear every timer and close every server. A single test file must finish well under 300 s in CI (the per-file limit): keep process-spawning tests few and fast, and prefer starting the CLI from a current `dist/` the way `cli/test/helpers/audit_fixture.ts` `cliEntry()` does.\n- Stress every new async or process test: run each new file 3 times in a row; fix flakes (tight deadlines are the usual cause; this host is under load from other work, so use generous waits and polling, not sleeps).\n- `cd cli && npx tsc --noEmit -p .` must be clean. `cli/node_modules` is a symlink; do not run npm install or cargo; do not change Rust.\n- Before each commit run, one at a time: every new test file; `leak_guard`; `protocol_parity`; `protocol_schema`; `check_requirements`; `release_workflow_parity`; `operation_schema`, `operation_lock`, `operation_entry_integrity`, `operation_runner_restart`, `goal_lifecycle` (if you touched operations); `context_convergence`; `node scripts/check_invariants.mjs`; `node scripts/check_requirements.mjs`; `node scripts/check_doc_versions.mjs`; the complexity gate. If the repo requires a ledger or doc entry for a new command (`scripts/doc_commands.mjs` is run by the package smoke test against the user manual), add it. `doctor.test.ts` fails 11 tests on this host on the base branch too: if you touch doctor, compare against `origin/release/v1.3.0` and report only differences.\n- When you need to undo an experiment, copy the file aside first and restore from the copy; do not use `git checkout <file>` while you have uncommitted work in it.\n- Preserve existing comments and unrelated code. Comments explain why, briefly, in plain sentences. Minimum code that meets the plan; nothing speculative.\n- Shell is zsh (use `bash -c '...'` for loops; `$PIPESTATUS` is empty in zsh); a hook filters output through `rtk` (prefix with `rtk proxy` if grep/sed output looks odd).\n\nReport (under 600 words): per slice: done / not done, the commit hash, files added and changed, tests added (names) with exact pass counts and the 3-run stress result, gates run; how the acceptance (\"kill the CLI; the run continues and resumes\") is demonstrated and by which test; each security property and the test that proves it; deviations from the plan and why; what is left for slice 3 or later; anything a release note must mention."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01QZVdHQcYW1UMq5KFk39Qf3","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"Implement HK-1 hooks","subagent_type":"general-purpose","prompt":"Implement unit HK-1 (hooks: pre-tool and post-tool) of the Nexus Code v1.3.0 plan, following the written design exactly: `/home/mike/nexus-wt/_plans/HK_PLAN.md`. Read the whole plan first (the shared design, the HK-1 sections, the security analysis, files, tests, docs, slices and \"Decisions taken\" are your spec; HK-2 and HK-3 are NOT your job except for shared machinery the plan assigns to an HK-1 slice). Work ONLY in the git worktree `/home/mike/nexus-wt/hk1` (branch `v1/hk1-hooks-lib`, cut from `origin/release/v1.3.0`). Do not push, open PRs, or touch any other worktree or `~/nexus-code` itself. Do not ask questions; where the plan leaves a detail open choose the conservative (fail-closed) option and list it in your report.\n\nThe unit's acceptance, from `docs/v1.3.0/PLAN.md`: \"a hook's allow has no effect\" (a hook can deny or annotate; it can never allow something a gate denied, and never change the tool input). Gate G-3: a hook must not be able to bypass a gate.\n\nDeliver the plan's slices in order, COMMITTING LOCALLY at the end of each slice (one commit per slice, message `feat(hooks): <what> (HK-1, slice N)` ending with the line `Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.2 <noreply@anthropic.com>`), each independently green:\n- Slice 1 (library): the hook runner (subprocess, timeout, stdin JSON, bounded output, environment as the plan specifies), the content-hash trust store for project hooks, the hooks file schema and its ledger entry, with the plan's tests.\n- Slice 2 (wiring): pre-tool attached to the spine's `contextAdmission` stage and post-tool to `evidenceEmission`, as the plan says, WITHOUT editing `dispatchToolCallImpl`, `run_turn.ts` or `sidecar.ts`. The plan notes this touches `cli/src/agent.ts`, which another agent has a claim on: keep the edit to one small self-contained block. Tests: a hook's \"allow\" and its `updatedInput` have no effect; `ask` counts as deny; a pre-tool hook error or timeout denies (fail closed); an invalid operator hooks file denies every call; an unapproved or changed project hooks file does not run; a denial is visible in the tool receipt and the flight log with the hook named; a hook cannot see secrets it should not (check what the plan says about the environment and redaction); a gate denial (approval refused, read-only mode, managed policy) stays a denial whatever a hook returns.\n- Slice 3 (`nexus hooks` commands, docs with a runnable example for gate G-6, doctor finding) ONLY if slices 1 and 2 are complete, committed and green. If you run short, stop cleanly after a finished slice and say so; never leave a half-wired command or a setting the docs describe as enforced when it is not.\n\nHard constraints:\n- No surface here may weaken an approval gate, verification-path protection, managed policy (`cli/src/core/policy_runtime.ts`) or the VERIFIED outcome ladder.\n- A CI gate fails if `runTurnImpl`, `dispatchToolCallImpl`, `runSubagent`, `compactNowImpl` or `driveOperation` grow in cyclomatic complexity or nesting (`cli/critical-complexity-baseline.json`). Do not add branches there; run the gate from the worktree root: `node cli/node_modules/tsx/dist/cli.mjs cli/scripts/check_critical_complexity.ts`.\n- Do not add or lengthen model-facing tool descriptions or the tool list (`cli/test/context_convergence.test.ts`, `cli/test/lsp_shape.test.ts` pin them). Hooks are invisible to the model except through a denial message.\n- Other agents hold file claims on `cli/src/index.ts`, `cli/src/doctor.ts`, `cli/src/config.ts`, `cli/src/types.ts`, and GV-5 work is editing `cli/src/mcp.ts` and the plugin loader. Edit those only with small self-contained additions (one register call; a doctor finding in its own function); put implementations in new files.\n- A hook is a program the operator (or an approved project file) names: run it without a shell where the plan allows, with a hard timeout, killing its whole process tree by pid on timeout (check pid + kernel start time before signalling, never `pkill`), bounded stdout/stderr, and never with the tool's secrets or the gateway key in its environment unless the plan says otherwise.\n- Every new on-disk format gets a JSON schema and a version ledger entry where the repo keeps them (`docs/protocol/` and its tests); earlier frozen schema versions stay byte-identical. If you change the sidecar protocol schema, regenerate `cli/test/fixtures/protocol_v1_schema_samples.jsonl` with `node cli/scripts/gen_protocol_samples.mjs`.\n- Linux x86_64 only; code must work on Node 22 and 24 (only 24 is installed here).\n\nWorking rules (mandatory):\n- Tests: `cd /home/mike/nexus-wt/hk1/cli && HOME=$(mktemp -d) node --import tsx --test test/<name>.test.ts 2>&1 | tail -30` (the runner prints `ℹ pass` / `ℹ fail`), one file at a time, foreground. A throwaway HOME is mandatory: never read or write the real `~/.nexus`, `/etc/nexus`, `~/.config/nexus`. No live inference (scripted gateway: `cli/test/helpers/fixtures.ts` `startGateway`), no network.\n- New test files import `./helpers/leak_guard.js` first; clear every timer; stop every child by its own pid in a `finally`. A test file must finish well under 300 s. Stress each new file: 3 runs in a row; this host is under load, so use generous waits and polling rather than tight sleeps.\n- `cd cli && npx tsc --noEmit -p .` must be clean. `cli/node_modules` is a symlink; do not run npm install or cargo; do not change Rust.\n- Before each commit run, one at a time: every new test file; `leak_guard`; `approval_policy`; `protocol_parity`; `protocol_schema`; `context_convergence`; `check_requirements`; the existing spine/dispatch tests (find them: `cli/test/*spine*`, `*dispatch*`, `mcp_tool_through_spine`); `sandbox_denial_events`; `hash_chain`; `node scripts/check_invariants.mjs`; `node scripts/check_requirements.mjs`; `node scripts/check_doc_versions.mjs`; the complexity gate. If the repo requires a ledger or doc entry for a new command (`scripts/doc_commands.mjs`), add it. `doctor.test.ts` fails 11 tests on this host on the base branch too: if you touch doctor, compare against `origin/release/v1.3.0` and report only differences.\n- For every guard, show by mutation that a test fails without it (copy the source file aside and restore from the copy; do not use `git checkout <file>` while you have uncommitted work in it), and read the failure message to make sure it fails for the right reason.\n- Preserve existing comments and unrelated code. Comments explain why, briefly, in plain sentences. Minimum code that meets the plan; nothing speculative.\n- Shell is zsh (use `bash -c '...'` for loops; `$PIPESTATUS` is empty in zsh); a hook filters output through `rtk` (prefix with `rtk proxy` if grep/sed output looks odd).\n\nReport (under 600 words): per slice: done / not done, the commit hash, files added and changed, tests added (names) with exact pass counts and the 3-run stress result, gates run; how the acceptance (\"a hook's allow has no effect\") is demonstrated and by which tests; each security property with the test and the mutation that proves it; deviations from the plan and why; what is left; anything a release note must mention."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}]}}
