{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-11-24T09:34:46.984Z","sessionId":"43bcbb67-2cff-4fa8-a05e-f8aa598df73b","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>acc00870f026d0f74</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>toolu_01FyFjrmv8VBGE8uDo2FKzzk</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>/tmp/claude-1000/-home-mike-nexus-code/d25d0d24-8aed-4b03-a951-966dc54f0666/tasks/acc00870f026d0f74.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Plan PF-3, PF-4, SB-1, MT-2, PA-2\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>I've written the plan to `/home/mike/nexus-wt/_plans/PF_SB_MT_PA_PLAN.md`, read against `origin/release/v1.3.0` at `4ae25a10`. No code was edited and nothing was run apart from the complexity checker.\n\n**Slices** (hot files marked; \"claimed\" means a live claim was held on it on 2026-11-24)\n\n- **PF-4, library entry point**\n  - Slice 1 (M): `lib.ts`, `sidecar_client.ts`, `tsconfig.lib.json`, `package.json`, `smoke-package.sh`, tests, `EMBEDDING.md` and a lib API ledger. No hot file.\n  - Slice 2 (S): the `examples/embed/` example plus `examples.test.ts` (claimed).\n- **SB-1, permission profiles**\n  - Slice 1 (S): new pure `sandbox_profile.ts` and its tests.\n  - Slice 2 (M): wiring. Hot: `config.ts` and `types.ts` (both claimed), plus `sandbox_network.ts` and `sandbox.ts`.\n  - Slice 3 (M): showing the profile. Hot: `doctor.ts` (claimed), `sidecar.ts`, `dispatch_tool_call.ts` (one property), `live_view.rs` (claimed).\n- **PF-3, socket transport**\n  - Slice 1 (M): the socket hub, protocol additions, schema and ledger. Hot: `sidecar.ts`, `protocol.ts`.\n  - Slice 2 (M): the `nexus serve` command, attach from the lib, the sandbox mask, docs and example. Hot: `index.ts` (2 lines, claimed), `examples.test.ts`.\n- **MT-2, tool registry**\n  - Slice 1 (S/M): characterization tests only, no source changes.\n  - Slice 2 (M): the registry. Hot: `agent.ts` and `spine_handlers.ts` (claimed).\n  - Slice 3 (L, three PRs): split `dispatch_tool_call.ts` into phases.\n  - Slice 4 (S): move the complexity baseline forward.\n- **PA-2, verified merges**\n  - Slice 1 (M): `operation_git.ts` and its tests. No hot file.\n  - Slice 2 (M): runner, schema v5 and the operation view. Hot: `operation_runner.ts` (claimed by Codex for PA-3) and `operation_schema.ts`.\n\n**Build order**\n1. PF-4 slice 1.\n2. SB-1 slice 1 and MT-2 slice 1: they touch no existing source and protect later work.\n3. PA-2 slice 1 (unclaimed and self-contained).\n4. PF-4 slice 2 and SB-1 slices 2–3, as their claims free up.\n5. PF-3, built on profiles and the client lib.\n6. PA-2 slice 2, after the PA-3 lane releases.\n7. MT-2 slices 2–4 last, after the Codex lanes on `agent.ts`, `spine_handlers.ts` and `run_turn.ts` release, with a short freeze on the dispatch files.\n\n**Decisions taken**\n- **PF-4:** no publish (D13). The package name stays `local-code-harness`, with `\"private\": true`. The lib only drives the sidecar as a separate process and does not export `ApplicationCore`, so it cannot skip the startup checks. The client API cannot send allow-always, and approvals it isn't told to grant are denied.\n- **PF-3:** unix socket only, with no TCP or HTTP listener, so the origin check has nothing to apply to. The acceptance wording only names the socket, the token and mode 0600, so it is still met. Only the same user can connect. The token is 256 bits, kept in a 0600 file and compared in constant time.\n- **PF-3 clients:** one controller and any number of read-only observers; only the controller answers approvals. On the socket, allow-always becomes allow-once, and `/mode auto`, `!`, `/cd` and `provider.*` are refused.\n- **PF-3 approvals:** a pending approval is re-sent when a controller reattaches. It is denied after 600 seconds with no controller.\n- **PF-3 startup:** `nexus serve` refuses auto approval and unconfined commands.\n- **SB-1:** three profiles, selected through `--sandbox [engine]:profile`. There is no new flag and no new managed-policy key. The doctor schema stays at version 2.\n- **MT-2:** `dispatchToolCallImpl` keeps its name and file. The complexity baseline moves forward only once, after the last slice, and only with your acknowledgement.\n- **PA-2:**\n  - After each merge, it re-runs every verifier already integrated plus the operator's verifier, capped at 16 distinct checks.\n  - A failing check is re-run on the pre-merge tree to decide whether the new unit caused it.\n  - A unit that edits another integrated unit's test files is refused.\n  - The operation schema goes to v5.\n  - \"Neither is integrated silently\" means the first unit lands with a verified record and the second is refused with a decision naming both units.\n\n**Main risks**\n- `dispatchToolCallImpl` has no complexity headroom: any added branch, including in SB-1 slice 3, fails CI.\n- Whether TypeScript 7 can emit the lib's type declarations is untested; the plan includes a hand-written fallback. `\"private\": true` might change the license notices output and break the byte-for-byte build check.\n- Contention with Codex claims on the hot files.\n- Allowing a socket client to reattach and answer a pending approval is new behaviour.\n- PA-2 adds verifier runs on every merge, and those checks run unsandboxed on the host.\n- `agent.ts` is at 4,967 lines, 33 under MT-1's 5,000-line limit.\n\n**Could not determine**\n- Whether TypeScript 7 supports declaration emit here.\n- Whether the PA-3/PA-4 lanes also plan an operation schema bump.\n- Which `security.ts` function PA-2 should reuse to classify verification files.\n- Whether any test depends on the full list of operation event types; probably not, because `WORK_INTEGRATED` only appears in `operation.ts` and the runner.\n- MT-1 is done (it brought `agent.ts` under 5,000 lines), but no acceptance test for it exists; MT-2 slice 1 adds one. Its BG-1 clause can't be checked until BG-1 exists.\n- Node can't read the connecting process's user ID, so file permissions are the only check on who connects.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>73454</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>120</tool_uses><duration_ms>2153919</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
