{"parentUuid":"58578f49-6622-4516-82e0-e92fd9a356cd","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-fable-5-2","id":"msg_011ChMYJQY5U8hG4Vitn33dD","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I pushed the test-timing fix to #777 and am posting the three review results now, then waiting on #777's checks.\n\n","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":693,"cache_read_input_tokens":843915,"output_tokens":3911,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":693,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":3911,"cache_read_input_tokens":843915,"cache_creation_input_tokens":693,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":693},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"thinkingDurationMs":5,"thinkingDisplay":"updates","apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011ChMYJQGSqkjTCxhN4fAc7","requestRef":"acd382dd-bcb0-4f5a-ac75-60b2598eb657","requestedModel":"claude-fable-5-2","promptCacheRequested":"1h","type":"assistant","uuid":"7805bf09-c1dd-4660-9843-1a193b397d51","timestamp":"2026-11-24T09:46:44.726Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-2","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","madeBy":"model","session_id":"d25d0d24-8aed-4b03-a951-966dc54f0666","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/mike/nexus-code","sessionId":"43bcbb67-2cff-4fa8-a05e-f8aa598df73b","version":"2.1.329","gitBranch":"main","slug":"synthetic-moseying-seahorse","dataFlowOn":true}
{"parentUuid":"7805bf09-c1dd-4660-9843-1a193b397d51","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-fable-5-2","id":"msg_011ChMYJQY5U8hG4Vitn33dD","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01XTv4rpVYUAXxjzwP4gVEQG","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/tmp/claude-1000/-home-mike-nexus-code/43bcbb67-2cff-4fa8-a05e-f8aa598df73b/scratchpad/rev777.md","content":"**Independent review at `d4117b8668715da096b10e107ce6293d546f75dc` — ACCEPT WITH FOLLOW-UPS, no blockers. One change since (`3d5f4350`): a longer wait in the recorded-client replay test and a correction to the evidence document, both described at the end.**\n\n**Checked**\n\n- **The pin.** The key is `fs.realpathSync` through `canonicalCwd` (symlinks, `..`, trailing slashes), and it is the same string later passed as the worktree. `session/new` and `session/load` share `startSession`. Two `session/new` on one directory cannot race: the pin is a synchronous call with no `await` between the map's get and set. A failed pin is stored and reused; at the cap nothing is evicted or pinned again. `pinBase` runs `rev-parse` through the hardened git wrapper, which runs no repository-configured program.\n- **Result fields** come from the same object that is passed to the service. Refusals and service errors return a JSON-RPC error with no result. Schemas v1 to v3 are byte-identical to the base; the generator's check reports v4 current.\n- **Receipts.** Only an explicit id reaches the service's receipt lookup, which is bound to the session; a verification no longer records itself as the session's latest receipt.\n- **Mutations:** removing the verify-during-prompt check, the pin reuse, or the receipt separation each fails `acp_verify_followups`.\n- **The test-timing change** weakens no assertion: one expectation changed with the behaviour (id-less get with no turn receipt is now -32602), and assertions for the new fields were added.\n\n**Follow-ups, none blocking**\n\n1. A client can deny verification of a directory for the life of the process: by opening a session on it before it has a commit, or by filling the sixteen pin slots with throwaway directories (a failed `session/load` uses a slot too). Stdio serves one client, so the only party affected is that client's own user. `docs/ACP.md` describes the mechanics and should name this consequence.\n2. The pin is per canonical directory, not per repository: a moved or freshly linked copy gets a fresh pin. The same-user limit already covers it, and a subdirectory is not a way round (`core/verify.ts:860-863` refuses a worktree that is not the top of the tree).\n3. The result's `workspace` is the receipt's, resolved again when the receipt is issued; a directory renamed mid-run could make it differ from the session's.\n4. Not probed by running: `$/cancel_request`, `session/cancel` and connection close during a verification; a real SIGTERM during one.\n\n**Tests at `d4117b86`:** `acp_verify_followups` 8, `acp_review_fixes` 9, `acp_stdio` 9, `acp_validate` 15, `acp_schema` 7, `acp_mapping` 22, `acp_jsonrpc` 11, `verify_service` 10, `leak_guard` 6, all passing; `tsc`, the schema check and the complexity gate pass.\n\n**`acp_client_conformance`** (the recorded Zed and CodeCompanion sessions) failed 0 of 2 for the reviewer at this head and at the release tip alike, with the turn timing out. It is not caused by this PR. On the same host I then saw 2/2 in six runs and 1/2 in two, under a load average of 13 to 15. Its wait for a real turn was 10 s; `3d5f4350` makes it 45 s, the same change as in `acp_stdio.test.ts`, and the evidence document now says the test is timing-sensitive instead of quoting a bare 2/2. One of my failures came after the longer wait, so the wait is not the whole story; I have recorded it as a flake to investigate and it does not block this PR, since CI runs it on every leg.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":693,"cache_read_input_tokens":843915,"output_tokens":3911,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":693,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":3911,"cache_read_input_tokens":843915,"cache_creation_input_tokens":693,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":693},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"toolInputStreamMs":12822,"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01XTv4rpVYUAXxjzwP4gVEQG":{"file_path":"/tmp/claude-1000/-home-mike-nexus-code/43bcbb67-2cff-4fa8-a05e-f8aa598df73b/scratchpad/rev777.md","content":"**Independent review at `d4117b8668715da096b10e107ce6293d546f75dc` — ACCEPT WITH FOLLOW-UPS, no blockers. One change since (`3d5f4350`): a longer wait in the recorded-client replay test and a correction to the evidence document, both described at the end.**\n\n**Checked**\n\n- **The pin.** The key is `fs.realpathSync` through `canonicalCwd` (symlinks, `..`, trailing slashes), and it is the same string later passed as the worktree. `session/new` and `session/load` share `startSession`. Two `session/new` on one directory cannot race: the pin is a synchronous call with no `await` between the map's get and set. A failed pin is stored and reused; at the cap nothing is evicted or pinned again. `pinBase` runs `rev-parse` through the hardened git wrapper, which runs no repository-configured program.\n- **Result fields** come from the same object that is passed to the service. Refusals and service errors return a JSON-RPC error with no result. Schemas v1 to v3 are byte-identical to the base; the generator's check reports v4 current.\n- **Receipts.** Only an explicit id reaches the service's receipt lookup, which is bound to the session; a verification no longer records itself as the session's latest receipt.\n- **Mutations:** removing the verify-during-prompt check, the pin reuse, or the receipt separation each fails `acp_verify_followups`.\n- **The test-timing change** weakens no assertion: one expectation changed with the behaviour (id-less get with no turn receipt is now -32602), and assertions for the new fields were added.\n\n**Follow-ups, none blocking**\n\n1. A client can deny verification of a directory for the life of the process: by opening a session on it before it has a commit, or by filling the sixteen pin slots with throwaway directories (a failed `session/load` uses a slot too). Stdio serves one client, so the only party affected is that client's own user. `docs/ACP.md` describes the mechanics and should name this consequence.\n2. The pin is per canonical directory, not per repository: a moved or freshly linked copy gets a fresh pin. The same-user limit already covers it, and a subdirectory is not a way round (`core/verify.ts:860-863` refuses a worktree that is not the top of the tree).\n3. The result's `workspace` is the receipt's, resolved again when the receipt is issued; a directory renamed mid-run could make it differ from the session's.\n4. Not probed by running: `$/cancel_request`, `session/cancel` and connection close during a verification; a real SIGTERM during one.\n\n**Tests at `d4117b86`:** `acp_verify_followups` 8, `acp_review_fixes` 9, `acp_stdio` 9, `acp_validate` 15, `acp_schema` 7, `acp_mapping` 22, `acp_jsonrpc` 11, `verify_service` 10, `leak_guard` 6, all passing; `tsc`, the schema check and the complexity gate pass.\n\n**`acp_client_conformance`** (the recorded Zed and CodeCompanion sessions) failed 0 of 2 for the reviewer at this head and at the release tip alike, with the turn timing out. It is not caused by this PR. On the same host I then saw 2/2 in six runs and 1/2 in two, under a load average of 13 to 15. Its wait for a real turn was 10 s; `3d5f4350` makes it 45 s, the same change as in `acp_stdio.test.ts`, and the evidence document now says the test is timing-sensitive instead of quoting a bare 2/2. One of my failures came after the longer wait, so the wait is not the whole story; I have recorded it as a flake to investigate and it does not block this PR, since CI runs it on every leg.\n"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011ChMYJQGSqkjTCxhN4fAc7","requestRef":"acd382dd-bcb0-4f5a-ac75-60b2598eb657","requestedModel":"claude-fable-5-2","promptCacheRequested":"1h","type":"assistant","uuid":"e18c39fb-f6bd-44a4-a71e-f382daf7a4c0","timestamp":"2026-11-24T09:46:57.575Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-2","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","madeBy":"model","session_id":"d25d0d24-8aed-4b03-a951-966dc54f0666","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/mike/nexus-code","sessionId":"43bcbb67-2cff-4fa8-a05e-f8aa598df73b","version":"2.1.329","gitBranch":"main","slug":"synthetic-moseying-seahorse","dataFlowOn":true}
{"parentUuid":"e18c39fb-f6bd-44a4-a71e-f382daf7a4c0","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"7527f3f2-461b-49ec-b4c9-bee1af3d2d6e","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XTv4rpVYUAXxjzwP4gVEQG","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /tmp/claude-1000/-home-mike-nexus-code/43bcbb67-2cff-4fa8-a05e-f8aa598df73b/scratchpad/rev777.md (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"9c943894-5e51-4b65-9dde-f78a07ddcda9","timestamp":"2026-11-24T09:46:59.700Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"create","filePath":"/tmp/claude-1000/-home-mike-nexus-code/43bcbb67-2cff-4fa8-a05e-f8aa598df73b/scratchpad/rev777.md","content":"**Independent review at `d4117b8668715da096b10e107ce6293d546f75dc` — ACCEPT WITH FOLLOW-UPS, no blockers. One change since (`3d5f4350`): a longer wait in the recorded-client replay test and a correction to the evidence document, both described at the end.**\n\n**Checked**\n\n- **The pin.** The key is `fs.realpathSync` through `canonicalCwd` (symlinks, `..`, trailing slashes), and it is the same string later passed as the worktree. `session/new` and `session/load` share `startSession`. Two `session/new` on one directory cannot race: the pin is a synchronous call with no `await` between the map's get and set. A failed pin is stored and reused; at the cap nothing is evicted or pinned again. `pinBase` runs `rev-parse` through the hardened git wrapper, which runs no repository-configured program.\n- **Result fields** come from the same object that is passed to the service. Refusals and service errors return a JSON-RPC error with no result. Schemas v1 to v3 are byte-identical to the base; the generator's check reports v4 current.\n- **Receipts.** Only an explicit id reaches the service's receipt lookup, which is bound to the session; a verification no longer records itself as the session's latest receipt.\n- **Mutations:** removing the verify-during-prompt check, the pin reuse, or the receipt separation each fails `acp_verify_followups`.\n- **The test-timing change** weakens no assertion: one expectation changed with the behaviour (id-less get with no turn receipt is now -32602), and assertions for the new fields were added.\n\n**Follow-ups, none blocking**\n\n1. A client can deny verification of a directory for the life of the process: by opening a session on it before it has a commit, or by filling the sixteen pin slots with throwaway directories (a failed `session/load` uses a slot too). Stdio serves one client, so the only party affected is that client's own user. `docs/ACP.md` describes the mechanics and should name this consequence.\n2. The pin is per canonical directory, not per repository: a moved or freshly linked copy gets a fresh pin. The same-user limit already covers it, and a subdirectory is not a way round (`core/verify.ts:860-863` refuses a worktree that is not the top of the tree).\n3. The result's `workspace` is the receipt's, resolved again when the receipt is issued; a directory renamed mid-run could make it differ from the session's.\n4. Not probed by running: `$/cancel_request`, `session/cancel` and connection close during a verification; a real SIGTERM during one.\n\n**Tests at `d4117b86`:** `acp_verify_followups` 8, `acp_review_fixes` 9, `acp_stdio` 9, `acp_validate` 15, `acp_schema` 7, `acp_mapping` 22, `acp_jsonrpc` 11, `verify_service` 10, `leak_guard` 6, all passing; `tsc`, the schema check and the complexity gate pass.\n\n**`acp_client_conformance`** (the recorded Zed and CodeCompanion sessions) failed 0 of 2 for the reviewer at this head and at the release tip alike, with the turn timing out. It is not caused by this PR. On the same host I then saw 2/2 in six runs and 1/2 in two, under a load average of 13 to 15. Its wait for a real turn was 10 s; `3d5f4350` makes it 45 s, the same change as in `acp_stdio.test.ts`, and the evidence document now says the test is timing-sensitive instead of quoting a bare 2/2. One of my failures came after the longer wait, so the wait is not the whole story; I have recorded it as a flake to investigate and it does not block this PR, since CI runs it on every leg.\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"e18c39fb-f6bd-44a4-a71e-f382daf7a4c0","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"session_id":"d25d0d24-8aed-4b03-a951-966dc54f0666","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/mike/nexus-code","sessionId":"43bcbb67-2cff-4fa8-a05e-f8aa598df73b","version":"2.1.329","gitBranch":"main","slug":"synthetic-moseying-seahorse","dataFlowOn":true}
{"parentUuid":"9c943894-5e51-4b65-9dde-f78a07ddcda9","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-fable-5-2","id":"msg_011ChMYJQY5U8hG4Vitn33dD","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01AVzFHutLecpHs56bWjHQ2m","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd ~/nexus-code && gh pr comment 777 --body-file /tmp/claude-1000/-home-mike-nexus-code/43bcbb67-2cff-4fa8-a05e-f8aa598df73b/scratchpad/rev777.md | tail -1; gh pr comment 774 --body \"**Independent review at \\`9e9366d8686ea37437ff26f47e9b67583c462c9a\\` — REQUEST CHANGES (one blocker: a stale generated schema)**\n\n**Blocking**\n- \\`docs/protocol/sidecar-v1.schema.json\\` is out of date: the new \\`TurnResult\\` fields make \\`protocol_schema\\` fail 1 of 8 (\\\"checked-in protocol schema equals a fresh generation\\\"). \\`node cli/scripts/gen_protocol_schema.mjs\\` adds 12 lines and its \\`--check\\` fails here. Regenerate it and record the version decision in \\`docs/protocol/VERSIONS.json\\`. (The samples fixture is current: \\`gen_protocol_samples --check\\` passes.)\n\n**Not blocking**\n- The admission check is not pinned by a test: making \\`initialTaskTimeStopReason\\` return \\`undefined\\` leaves all 6 tests green, because the loop-top \\`Date.now() > turnDeadline\\` check catches the same case. With a zero remainder that backstop uses \\`>\\` on the same millisecond, so one step could still dispatch. A test with a frozen clock would settle it.\n- A backwards clock step inside a process exhausts the whole budget (\\`finishTaskElapsedBudget\\` sets elapsed to the maximum when now is before \\`taskActiveSinceMs\\`). It fails closed, but a small NTP correction ends a long objective for good. A monotonic delta within a process, wall clock only across processes, would avoid that.\n- A legacy objective with no counter is charged \\`now − startedAt\\`, so any objective older than the default (4,320 minutes) is exhausted at its first resume. Deliberate and conservative; the docs should say so.\n- What is counted: running intervals, plus downtime after a crash or kill (an open interval is charged through the downtime, so a kill followed by a resume a week later exhausts the budget). Idle time between cleanly ended turns is not counted. I found no operator-facing text stating this.\n- \\`NEXUS_MAX_TASK_MINUTES\\` is in \\`PROJECT_ENV_KEYS\\`, so a project \\`.env\\` can set it (a resume keeps the lower of saved and new, so it can only lower an existing ceiling). Say so in the docs, as #765 did for the submission budget.\n\n**Held up**\n- Resetting the counter on resume fails the \\\"can only be lowered on resume\\\" test. The start of the active interval is saved before any dispatch, and a failed save blocks inference.\n- One timer for the lower of the turn deadline and the task remainder, cleared on both exits.\n- Complexity gate: \\`runTurnImpl\\` CC 324→324, nesting 5→5 (lines 1,262→1,290, which the gate reports and does not enforce).\n- The run-result schema change is additive with a ledger entry; the new test file registers the leak guard.\n\n**Tests at this head:** \\`p10_task_elapsed_budget\\` 6/6, \\`p10_inference_submission_budget\\` 4/4, \\`turn_deadline_abort\\` 3/3, \\`protocol_parity\\` 5/5, \\`run_json_contract\\` 15/15, \\`check_requirements\\` 28/28, \\`leak_guard\\` 6/6, \\`tsc\\` clean; \\`protocol_schema\\` 7 pass, 1 fail.\n\n**Not checked:** Node 22, the save ordering under a real kill, the plan document beyond its first line.\" | tail -1; gh pr comment 775 --body \"**Independent review at \\`af6c50e046ba157d716313ebbe4ed8b62442a22a\\` — ACCEPT WITH FOLLOW-UPS. The head has since moved to \\`7945d57b\\`, which this does not cover.**\n\n**What holds:** the cap counts rolled-up total tokens per goal across its sub-operations (reported and estimated); at the cap the goal is \\`PAUSED\\` with a visible reason and is never \\`COMPLETE\\`. The cap is operator-controlled (\\`NEXUS_OPERATION_MAX_TOTAL_TOKENS\\` and the config key; the env key is not in \\`PROJECT_ENV_KEYS\\`, so a project \\`.env\\` cannot set it). It is optional, the schema field is optional, and a goal without it behaves as before. Replacing the cap condition with \\`false\\` fails the new test (7 pass, 1 fail). Complexity gate: \\`driveOperation\\` 17→17, nesting 3→3.\n\n**Follow-ups**\n1. The doc says \\\"Resume continues after the operator raises the cap\\\", but no operator path raises \\`policy.maxTotalTokens\\` on a saved goal: \\`resume\\` only clears the pause and the policy is fixed at creation, so a resume meets the cap again at once. Add the path or reword.\n2. The cap is checked before a unit is dispatched only, so one unit can overshoot without bound. The doc should say so.\n3. A failure to measure is silent: \\`rolledUpTokens\\` swallows a child state that cannot be loaded, and with no store nothing is enforced. Same posture as the existing usage code, and the doc states it; it fails open.\n4. \\`pauseForTokenBudget\\` has an empty \\`BLOCKED\\` branch.\n5. The test sets \\`operation.policy\\` directly: nothing covers the env or config path into the plan, the no-cap case, or resume after a raise.\n\n**Tests at \\`af6c50e0\\`:** \\`goal_lifecycle\\` 8/8, \\`goal_ledger_propagation\\` 2/2, \\`operation_entry_integrity\\` 3/3, \\`operation_schema\\` 18/18, \\`operation_runner_restart\\` 1/1, \\`protocol_parity\\` 5/5, \\`protocol_schema\\` 8/8, \\`run_json_contract\\` 15/15, \\`check_requirements\\` 28/28, \\`leak_guard\\` 6/6, \\`tsc\\` clean.\" | tail -1; echo \"$(date -u +%FT%TZ) REVIEWS (claude-invariants-audit): #777 (mine) ACCEPT WITH FOLLOW-UPS at d4117b86, posted; head now 3d5f4350 (test wait + evidence wording). #774 REQUEST CHANGES at 9e9366d8: docs/protocol/sidecar-v1.schema.json stale (protocol_schema fails 1/8; run gen_protocol_schema.mjs). #775 ACCEPT WITH FOLLOW-UPS at af6c50e0 (head moved to 7945d57b, not covered).\" >> \"$(git rev-parse --git-common-dir)/nexus-claims/nexus-claims.log\"","description":"Post the three reviews and log them"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":693,"cache_read_input_tokens":843915,"output_tokens":3911,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":693,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":3911,"cache_read_input_tokens":843915,"cache_creation_input_tokens":693,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":693},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"toolInputStreamMs":19359,"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01AVzFHutLecpHs56bWjHQ2m":{"command":"cd ~/nexus-code && gh pr comment 777 --body-file /tmp/claude-1000/-home-mike-nexus-code/43bcbb67-2cff-4fa8-a05e-f8aa598df73b/scratchpad/rev777.md | tail -1; gh pr comment 774 --body \"**Independent review at \\`9e9366d8686ea37437ff26f47e9b67583c462c9a\\` — REQUEST CHANGES (one blocker: a stale generated schema)**\n\n**Blocking**\n- \\`docs/protocol/sidecar-v1.schema.json\\` is out of date: the new \\`TurnResult\\` fields make \\`protocol_schema\\` fail 1 of 8 (\\\"checked-in protocol schema equals a fresh generation\\\"). \\`node cli/scripts/gen_protocol_schema.mjs\\` adds 12 lines and its \\`--check\\` fails here. Regenerate it and record the version decision in \\`docs/protocol/VERSIONS.json\\`. (The samples fixture is current: \\`gen_protocol_samples --check\\` passes.)\n\n**Not blocking**\n- The admission check is not pinned by a test: making \\`initialTaskTimeStopReason\\` return \\`undefined\\` leaves all 6 tests green, because the loop-top \\`Date.now() > turnDeadline\\` check catches the same case. With a zero remainder that backstop uses \\`>\\` on the same millisecond, so one step could still dispatch. A test with a frozen clock would settle it.\n- A backwards clock step inside a process exhausts the whole budget (\\`finishTaskElapsedBudget\\` sets elapsed to the maximum when now is before \\`taskActiveSinceMs\\`). It fails closed, but a small NTP correction ends a long objective for good. A monotonic delta within a process, wall clock only across processes, would avoid that.\n- A legacy objective with no counter is charged \\`now − startedAt\\`, so any objective older than the default (4,320 minutes) is exhausted at its first resume. Deliberate and conservative; the docs should say so.\n- What is counted: running intervals, plus downtime after a crash or kill (an open interval is charged through the downtime, so a kill followed by a resume a week later exhausts the budget). Idle time between cleanly ended turns is not counted. I found no operator-facing text stating this.\n- \\`NEXUS_MAX_TASK_MINUTES\\` is in \\`PROJECT_ENV_KEYS\\`, so a project \\`.env\\` can set it (a resume keeps the lower of saved and new, so it can only lower an existing ceiling). Say so in the docs, as #765 did for the submission budget.\n\n**Held up**\n- Resetting the counter on resume fails the \\\"can only be lowered on resume\\\" test. The start of the active interval is saved before any dispatch, and a failed save blocks inference.\n- One timer for the lower of the turn deadline and the task remainder, cleared on both exits.\n- Complexity gate: \\`runTurnImpl\\` CC 324→324, nesting 5→5 (lines 1,262→1,290, which the gate reports and does not enforce).\n- The run-result schema change is additive with a ledger entry; the new test file registers the leak guard.\n\n**Tests at this head:** \\`p10_task_elapsed_budget\\` 6/6, \\`p10_inference_submission_budget\\` 4/4, \\`turn_deadline_abort\\` 3/3, \\`protocol_parity\\` 5/5, \\`run_json_contract\\` 15/15, \\`check_requirements\\` 28/28, \\`leak_guard\\` 6/6, \\`tsc\\` clean; \\`protocol_schema\\` 7 pass, 1 fail.\n\n**Not checked:** Node 22, the save ordering under a real kill, the plan document beyond its first line.\" | tail -1; gh pr comment 775 --body \"**Independent review at \\`af6c50e046ba157d716313ebbe4ed8b62442a22a\\` — ACCEPT WITH FOLLOW-UPS. The head has since moved to \\`7945d57b\\`, which this does not cover.**\n\n**What holds:** the cap counts rolled-up total tokens per goal across its sub-operations (reported and estimated); at the cap the goal is \\`PAUSED\\` with a visible reason and is never \\`COMPLETE\\`. The cap is operator-controlled (\\`NEXUS_OPERATION_MAX_TOTAL_TOKENS\\` and the config key; the env key is not in \\`PROJECT_ENV_KEYS\\`, so a project \\`.env\\` cannot set it). It is optional, the schema field is optional, and a goal without it behaves as before. Replacing the cap condition with \\`false\\` fails the new test (7 pass, 1 fail). Complexity gate: \\`driveOperation\\` 17→17, nesting 3→3.\n\n**Follow-ups**\n1. The doc says \\\"Resume continues after the operator raises the cap\\\", but no operator path raises \\`policy.maxTotalTokens\\` on a saved goal: \\`resume\\` only clears the pause and the policy is fixed at creation, so a resume meets the cap again at once. Add the path or reword.\n2. The cap is checked before a unit is dispatched only, so one unit can overshoot without bound. The doc should say so.\n3. A failure to measure is silent: \\`rolledUpTokens\\` swallows a child state that cannot be loaded, and with no store nothing is enforced. Same posture as the existing usage code, and the doc states it; it fails open.\n4. \\`pauseForTokenBudget\\` has an empty \\`BLOCKED\\` branch.\n5. The test sets \\`operation.policy\\` directly: nothing covers the env or config path into the plan, the no-cap case, or resume after a raise.\n\n**Tests at \\`af6c50e0\\`:** \\`goal_lifecycle\\` 8/8, \\`goal_ledger_propagation\\` 2/2, \\`operation_entry_integrity\\` 3/3, \\`operation_schema\\` 18/18, \\`operation_runner_restart\\` 1/1, \\`protocol_parity\\` 5/5, \\`protocol_schema\\` 8/8, \\`run_json_contract\\` 15/15, \\`check_requirements\\` 28/28, \\`leak_guard\\` 6/6, \\`tsc\\` clean.\" | tail -1; echo \"$(date -u +%FT%TZ) REVIEWS (claude-invariants-audit): #777 (mine) ACCEPT WITH FOLLOW-UPS at d4117b86, posted; head now 3d5f4350 (test wait + evidence wording). #774 REQUEST CHANGES at 9e9366d8: docs/protocol/sidecar-v1.schema.json stale (protocol_schema fails 1/8; run gen_protocol_schema.mjs). #775 ACCEPT WITH FOLLOW-UPS at af6c50e0 (head moved to 7945d57b, not covered).\" >> \"$(git rev-parse --git-common-dir)/nexus-claims/nexus-claims.log\"","description":"Post the three reviews and log them"}},"apiBlockIndex":2,"requestId":"req_011ChMYJQGSqkjTCxhN4fAc7","requestRef":"acd382dd-bcb0-4f5a-ac75-60b2598eb657","requestedModel":"claude-fable-5-2","promptCacheRequested":"1h","type":"assistant","uuid":"9b687d37-5af7-47ec-a932-488ff40a2b2c","timestamp":"2026-11-24T09:47:16.950Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-2","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","madeBy":"model","session_id":"d25d0d24-8aed-4b03-a951-966dc54f0666","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/mike/nexus-code","sessionId":"43bcbb67-2cff-4fa8-a05e-f8aa598df73b","version":"2.1.329","gitBranch":"main","slug":"synthetic-moseying-seahorse","dataFlowOn":true}
