{"parentUuid":"63455489-0f13-4ada-92d5-a530d521586f","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-fable-5-2","id":"msg_011ChMbtNvtNaS5Mqs4T6b29","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01TvGVs2kEssZaahxbkYYzbV","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"Review PR #778 policy enforcement","subagent_type":"reviewer","prompt":"Independent security review of GitHub PR #778 in the repo at ~/nexus-code (`gh pr view 778`, `gh pr diff 778`), \"feat(policy): enforce MCP and plugin allowlists, close the always-approve gap, add nexus policy (GV-1 slices 3-4, GV-5)\", base `release/v1.3.0`. Take the full head SHA from `gh pr view 778 --json headRefOid`, review exactly that, and check at the end whether it moved or was merged. Time-box: about 45 minutes. This host is under heavy load from other work: tests with tight timing may fail for that reason; when a test fails, rerun it once alone and, if it still fails, run the same file on `origin/release/v1.3.0` and report only differences. Prioritise sections 1–3 below; if you run short, give a verdict on what you covered and list the rest.\n\nContext: managed policy is an operator-controlled TOML policy in system and user tiers plus a project tier that can only tighten (`cli/src/core/managed_policy.ts` library, `managed_policy_load.ts`, `cli/src/core/policy_runtime.ts` enforcement, docs `docs/MANAGED_POLICY.md`, plan `/home/mike/nexus-wt/_plans/GV1_PLAN.md`). The invariant: policy can only restrict; an invalid or unreadable policy refuses rather than meaning \"no policy\"; nothing a model, a tool result or a workspace file says may loosen it. This PR enforces `mcp.allow`, `mcp.disable_workspace`, `plugins.allow`, `plugins.disable_workspace` (`cli/src/mcp.ts`, `cli/src/plugins.ts`, `cli/src/workspace_trust.ts`), stops an \"always\" approval answer and `/mode auto` from switching the session to auto under `approval.disable_auto`/`strict` (`cli/src/sidecar.ts`, `cli/src/repl.ts`, `cli/src/slash/session_commands.ts`), makes `nexus verify` refuse `--yes`, `--sandbox none`, `--sandbox-network` under policy (`verifyFlagRefusal` in `policy_runtime.ts`, called from `cli/src/index.ts`), and adds `nexus policy show|check` (`cli/src/policy_cli.ts`) and doctor findings (`cli/src/doctor.ts`).\n\nTry to break it, by running wherever practical (write probes as an extra untracked test file under `cli/test/` modelled on `cli/test/managed_policy_allowlists.test.ts`, `managed_policy_always.test.ts`, `policy_cli.test.ts`, and delete it afterwards; point the policy loader at temp paths the way those tests do):\n1. MCP allowlist bypasses: a server that is not on `mcp.allow` still getting started or its tools still being offered — by name tricks (case, whitespace, unicode look-alikes, a name equal to an allowed one but a different command), by transport (stdio vs HTTP vs WebSocket: the PR says WebSocket servers match by name only — can a workspace define a WebSocket/HTTP server with an allowed NAME pointing at an attacker URL? is that within what `mcp.allow` promises, and is it documented?), by the three entry paths (`loadAndConnect`, `addServer` via `/mcp add`, `addConnection`) and any fourth path you can find (plugins contributing MCP servers, `serve-mcp`, ACP `session/new` `mcpServers` — the ACP adapter does not start client MCP servers; confirm), by a config file reached through a symlink out of or into the workspace (`isWorkspaceFile`: real path vs given path, a symlinked parent directory, a hard link, `..`), by `disable_workspace=true` with the workspace being `$HOME` or a parent of the user config directory, and by a policy that is unreadable/invalid at start (must admit nothing) or that changes on disk mid-session (when is it read? a single frozen load per process is the stated design).\n2. Plugin allowlist: what identifies a plugin (path? sha256?), can a workspace plugin be loaded when `plugins.disable_workspace` is set through a symlink in the user plugin directory pointing into the workspace or the reverse; the hash-then-import race is documented as a limit — confirm the docs say so and that a mismatch refuses.\n3. The always-approve gap: under `approval.disable_auto` (and `strict`), drive the sidecar with an approval reply `{always: true}` and the REPL's \"always\" answer and `/mode auto`, and confirm the NEXT gated tool call asks again; check every other route to auto in the same process (a `slash` command through the sidecar, `--yes`, `NEXUS_APPROVAL_MODE=auto`, the ACP adapter's hardcoded `always:false`, subagents inheriting a mode, an operation/goal/batch runner setting a mode, `agent.setApprovalMode`): does any still switch to auto under the policy? Without the policy, \"always\" must behave as before (mutate to confirm the tests distinguish the two).\n4. `nexus verify` flag refusals: exit 2 with the source named; can the same effect be reached by env or config instead of the flags (e.g. an env that selects `--sandbox none`)?\n5. `nexus policy show|check`: they run even with a broken installed policy; can `check <file>` be pointed at a file to leak content it should not (it prints parse errors: of an arbitrary file? note if it echoes file contents), does it execute anything, and does `--json` output stay valid JSON on error? `nexus doctor --fix` must never write a `policy.toml`: test with a policy file that doctor would otherwise \"fix\".\n6. Behaviour change when no policy is present: everything must be exactly as before (MCP servers start, plugins load, \"always\" works). Run `cli/test/mcp.test.ts`, `mcp_tool_through_spine`, `approval_policy`, `sidecar` (slow: allow 9 minutes; expect load-related timeouts and compare with base), and the existing `managed_policy_*.test.ts` files.\n7. Mutations (copy the source aside, restore from the copy; never `git checkout` to undo): remove the MCP gate on one of the three paths; remove the plugin gate; make \"always\" stick under `disable_auto`; make `verifyFlagRefusal` return nothing. Each must fail a test; report which.\n8. Run the gates from the worktree root: `cd cli && npx tsc --noEmit -p .`; `node cli/node_modules/tsx/dist/cli.mjs cli/scripts/check_critical_complexity.ts`; `node scripts/check_invariants.mjs`; `node scripts/check_requirements.mjs`; and `cli/test/leak_guard.test.ts`, `context_convergence` (very slow under load: run it only if time allows and say if you did not).\n9. Docs: does `docs/MANAGED_POLICY.md` now say only what is enforced, including the limits (WebSocket by name, the hash-import race, `$HOME` as workspace)?\n\nMandatory rules:\n- Do NOT edit ~/nexus-code or existing worktrees. `git -C ~/nexus-code fetch origin && git -C ~/nexus-code worktree add --detach ~/nexus-wt/rev778 <full sha>`; `cp -a ~/nexus-code/cli/node_modules ~/nexus-wt/rev778/cli/`; `mkdir -p ~/nexus-wt/rev778/core/target/release && cp ~/nexus-wt/ls1b/core/target/release/{harness-core,nexus-tui} ~/nexus-wt/rev778/core/target/release/`. At the end delete probe files and run `git -C ~/nexus-code worktree remove --force ~/nexus-wt/rev778`. No commits, pushes or GitHub comments.\n- Tests: `cd ~/nexus-wt/rev778/cli && HOME=$(mktemp -d) node --import tsx --test test/<name>.test.ts 2>&1 | tail -20`, foreground, one at a time (the runner prints `ℹ pass` / `ℹ fail`). A throwaway HOME is mandatory. Never read the real ~/.nexus, /etc/nexus or ~/.config/nexus. No live inference, no network.\n- Stop every process you start by its own pid; never `pkill -f`. No background loops.\n- Shell is zsh (use `bash -c` for loops; `$PIPESTATUS` is empty in zsh); output passes through `rtk` (use `rtk proxy <cmd>` if it looks filtered).\n\nReport under 650 words: verdict (ACCEPT / ACCEPT WITH FOLLOW-UPS / REQUEST CHANGES) at the exact full SHA; bypasses found, ranked, each with file:line, confirmed-by-running or by-reading, blocking vs non-blocking; mutation results; test counts with base comparison where a file failed; gates; what you did not check."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2575,"cache_read_input_tokens":865004,"output_tokens":3158,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2575,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":3158,"cache_read_input_tokens":865004,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2575,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2575},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"toolInputStreamMs":29764,"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01TvGVs2kEssZaahxbkYYzbV":{"description":"Review PR #778 policy enforcement","subagent_type":"reviewer","prompt":"Independent security review of GitHub PR #778 in the repo at ~/nexus-code (`gh pr view 778`, `gh pr diff 778`), \"feat(policy): enforce MCP and plugin allowlists, close the always-approve gap, add nexus policy (GV-1 slices 3-4, GV-5)\", base `release/v1.3.0`. Take the full head SHA from `gh pr view 778 --json headRefOid`, review exactly that, and check at the end whether it moved or was merged. Time-box: about 45 minutes. This host is under heavy load from other work: tests with tight timing may fail for that reason; when a test fails, rerun it once alone and, if it still fails, run the same file on `origin/release/v1.3.0` and report only differences. Prioritise sections 1–3 below; if you run short, give a verdict on what you covered and list the rest.\n\nContext: managed policy is an operator-controlled TOML policy in system and user tiers plus a project tier that can only tighten (`cli/src/core/managed_policy.ts` library, `managed_policy_load.ts`, `cli/src/core/policy_runtime.ts` enforcement, docs `docs/MANAGED_POLICY.md`, plan `/home/mik