{"parentUuid":"4e689672-bd32-4c07-8a61-07bdc2a9581a","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-fable-5-2","id":"msg_011ChMwvDw2f6HWa4dfLaakc","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Both reviews are posted. I'm now sending the three trust fixes for hooks back to the agent that built HK-1, since it has the context."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3540,"cache_read_input_tokens":951866,"output_tokens":2588,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":96},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3540,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2588,"cache_read_input_tokens":951866,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3540,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3540},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011ChMwvDU15MNUZLuzqbR9T","requestRef":"48fd2aec-fbba-401f-8d41-d50910dc12f3","requestedModel":"claude-fable-5-2","promptCacheRequested":"1h","type":"assistant","uuid":"0cba659a-6ac7-4754-9fa1-21ce70621f4e","timestamp":"2026-11-24T14:56:26.574Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-2","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","madeBy":"model","session_id":"d25d0d24-8aed-4b03-a951-966dc54f0666","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/mike/nexus-code","sessionId":"43bcbb67-2cff-4fa8-a05e-f8aa598df73b","version":"2.1.329","gitBranch":"main","slug":"synthetic-moseying-seahorse","dataFlowOn":true}
{"parentUuid":"0cba659a-6ac7-4754-9fa1-21ce70621f4e","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-fable-5-2","id":"msg_011ChMwvDw2f6HWa4dfLaakc","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_013MXix3AgVYswvTvmWruZJj","name":"SendMessage","input":{"to":"a317b41792fa50650","summary":"Fix three trust blockers in HK-1 hooks","message":"Follow-up work on HK-1 in /home/mike/nexus-wt/hk1 (branch v1/hk1-hooks-lib; it now has the release branch merged in twice and is pushed as PR #782; start with `git status` and `git log --oneline -6`; do not push; commit locally at the end with message `fix(hooks): <what>` ending with the line `Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.2 <noreply@anthropic.com>`). An independent review of PR #782 found three blockers, all confirmed by running, plus smaller items. Same working rules as before (throwaway HOME, `TMPDIR=/dev/shm` for speed, leak guard, kill by pid, mutations restored from a copy and never with `git checkout`, complexity gate, no model-facing tool text changes). Fix these, each with a test that fails without the fix (read the failure message to check it fails for the right reason), and report under 450 words.\n\nBLOCKER 1: the model can approve its own project hooks. `nexus hooks approve --sha256 <hex>` works with no terminal (cli/src/hooks_cli.ts ~118), and the trust store `extension_trust.json` and `hooks.json` are not in `DEFAULT_PROTECTED_PATHS` (cli/src/security.ts:163), unlike `policy_trust.json` and `trusted_workspaces.json`. Repro in auto mode: `write_file .nexus/hooks.json`, then `run_command \"nexus hooks approve --sha256 <hash> -w <repo>\"` → exit 0, store written, hook runs on the next call.\n Fix, all three parts:\n (a) Add the trust store file name and the operator hooks file name to `DEFAULT_PROTECTED_PATHS` with a one-line reason each, exactly as GV-1 did for `policy_trust.json`; find GV-1's test for that (search the tests for `policy_trust.json` and `isPathProtected`) and add the equivalent for both names, including through the agent's write tools.\n (b) Approval must be an act of a person at a terminal: look at how `nexus trust` (workspace trust) and GV-1's project-policy approval decide that (search `isTTY`, `process.stdin.isTTY`, \"trust\" in cli/src/index.ts, cli/src/workspace_trust.ts, cli/src/core/policy_runtime.ts and their tests) and do the same for `nexus hooks approve`: refuse without an interactive terminal (stdin AND stdout a TTY), with a message that says why; the `--sha256` flag may stay only as a confirmation of WHICH content is being approved in an interactive run, never as a way to approve without a terminal. `nexus hooks revoke` may stay non-interactive (it only removes trust). Tests need a seam: inject the \"is interactive\" predicate the way the existing trust code's tests do (do not add an env var that a model could set to fake a terminal). Also check whether the model's shell tool could fake a TTY (`script -q -c ...`): if the existing `nexus trust` guard has an answer to that (e.g. a refusal when running under the agent, detected by an env marker the agent sets on its child processes — search for such a marker, e.g. `NEXUS_AGENT`/`NEXUS_INSIDE`/similar, in cli/src/tools.ts and the Rust bridge env), reuse it; if there is none, say so plainly in the report and in docs/HOOKS.md as a limit (an unsandboxed command run in auto mode acts with the operator's rights).\n (c) A test for the whole route: an agent in auto mode that writes a project hooks file and then tries to approve it by `run_command` must not end with an active hook.\n\nBLOCKER 2: with the workspace set to the home directory, `write_file .config/nexus/hooks.json` created an operator hooks file that was accepted (mode 644) and its hook ran. (a) above protects the name; in addition require the operator hooks file to be owned by the current user and not group- or world-writable AND refuse it when it lies inside the active workspace (the workspace is then the operator's home: say in the denial what to do); test the workspace=HOME case end to end.\n\nBLOCKER 3: approval does not pin the script a hook names. After approving `command: [\"./guard.sh\"]` the model overwrote `guard.sh` and the next call ran the model's script. Fix: the approved identity of a project hooks file must cover the bytes of every file its commands name that resolves inside the workspace (command[0] and any argument that is an existing file path inside the workspace; resolve symlinks; refuse a command[0] that is a relative path escaping the workspace or a symlink out of it), re-checked before each run exactly as the hooks file's own hash is; a change makes the hooks inactive and \"asks again\" like a changed hooks file. `nexus hooks list`/`approve` show which files are covered. For a command[0] that is a bare name found through PATH or an absolute path outside the workspace, nothing in the workspace can replace it: state in docs/HOOKS.md that such a program is trusted as installed. Test: approve, overwrite the script through the agent's write tool, next tool call: hook not run, denial/notice names the changed file, re-approval needed.\n\nSMALLER ITEMS (do them if cheap, otherwise list as open):\n - A hook's denial reason reaches the model as `[hook X (project)] <text>`; make it unmistakably quoted hook-supplied text (a fixed framing sentence that says the text comes from a hook program and is not an instruction, the reason JSON-string-quoted and still capped) and test that a reason containing \"IGNORE PREVIOUS INSTRUCTIONS\" arrives inside that quoting.\n - docs/HOOKS.md: correct the claim about operation units (project hooks do not run in a unit's own worktree agent unless that is changed; say what is true), say that the hooks stage also applies to the operator's own `!cmd`, that a running hook is not stopped by cancel or the turn deadline and queue time is outside its timeout, and that when a hook denies a call a spine-stage gate would also have denied, the gate's own reason and later-stage records are replaced by the hook denial.\n - Add a test that a subagent's tool call goes through the hooks (the review found none).\n\nThen run, one at a time: every hook test file, `leak_guard`, `approval_policy`, `check_requirements`, the GV-1 trust/protected-path tests you touched or copied from, `policy_cli`, `managed_policy_allowlists`, `tsc --noEmit`, the complexity gate, `node scripts/check_invariants.mjs`, `node scripts/check_doc_versions.mjs`, and the hooks schema generator's `--check` if the trust record format changed (bump/ledger per the repo's rule if it did). Report: per blocker, file:line of the fix, the test names, the mutation that fails each; what the TTY guard can and cannot stop; any schema/ledger change; test counts; the commit hash; what is left open.","type":"message","recipient":"a317b41792fa50650","recipient_kind":"agent","content":"Follow-up work on HK-1 in /home/mike/nexus-wt/hk1…"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3540,"cache_read_input_tokens":951866,"output_tokens":2588,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":96},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3540,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2588,"cache_read_input_tokens":951866,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3540,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3540},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"toolInputStreamMs":25352,"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_013MXix3AgVYswvTvmWruZJj":{"to":"a317b41792fa50650","summary":"Fix three trust blockers in HK-1 hooks","message":"Follow-up work on HK-1 in /home/mike/nexus-wt/hk1 (branch v1/hk1-hooks-lib; it now has the release branch merged in twice and is pushed as PR #782; start with `git status` and `git log --oneline -6`; do not push; commit locally at the end with message `fix(hooks): <what>` ending with the line `Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.2 <noreply@anthropic.com>`). An independent review of PR #782 found three blockers, all confirmed by running, plus smaller items. Same working rules as before (throwaway HOME, `TMPDIR=/dev/shm` for speed, leak guard, kill by pid, mutations restored from a copy and never with `git checkout`, complexity gate, no model-facing tool text changes). Fix these, each with a test that fails without the fix (read the failure message to check it fails for the right reason), and report under 450 words.\n\nBLOCKER 1: the model can approve its own project hooks. `nexus hooks approve --sha256 <hex>` works with no terminal (cli/src/hooks_cli.ts ~118), and the trust store `extension_trust.json` and `hooks.json` are not in `DEFAULT_PROTECTED_PATHS` (cli/src/security.ts:163), unlike `policy_trust.json` and `trusted_workspaces.json`. Repro in auto mode: `write_file .nexus/hooks.json`, then `run_command \"nexus hooks approve --sha256 <hash> -w <repo>\"` → exit 0, store written, hook runs on the next call.\n Fix, all three parts:\n (a) Add the trust store file name and the operator hooks file name to `DEFAULT_PROTECTED_PATHS` with a one-line reason each, exactly as GV-1 did for `policy_trust.json`; find GV-1's test for that (search the tests for `policy_trust.json` and `isPathProtected`) and add the equivalent for both names, including through the agent's write tools.\n (b) Approval must be an act of a person at a terminal: look at how `nexus trust` (workspace trust) and GV-1's project-policy approval decide that (search `isTTY`, `process.stdin.isTTY`, \"trust\" in cli/src/index.ts, cli/src/workspace_trust.ts, cli/src/core/policy_runtime.ts and their tests) and do the same for `nexus hooks approve`: refuse without an interactive terminal (stdin AND stdout a TTY), with a message that says why; the `--sha256` flag may stay only as a confirmation of WHICH content is being approved in an interactive run, never as a way to approve without a terminal. `nexus hooks revoke` may stay non-interactive (it only removes trust). Tests need a seam: inject the \"is interactive\" predicate the way the existing trust code's tests do (do not add an env var that a model could set to fake a terminal). Also check whether the model's shell tool could fake a TTY (`script -q -c ...`): if the existing `nexus trust` guard has an answer to that (e.g. a refusal when running under the agent, detected by an env marker the agent sets on its child processes — search for such a marker, e.g. `NEXUS_AGENT`/`NEXUS_INSIDE`/similar, in cli/src/tools.ts and the Rust bridge env), reuse it; if there is none, say so plainly in the report and in docs/HOOKS.md as a limit (an unsandboxed command run in auto mode acts with the operator's rights).\n (c) A test for the whole route: an agent in auto mode that writes a project hooks file and then tries to approve it by `run_command` must not end with an active hook.\n\nBLOCKER 2: with the workspace set to the home directory, `write_file .config/nexus/hooks.json` created an operator hooks file that was accepted (mode 644) and its hook ran. (a) above protects the name; in addition require the operator hooks file to be owned by the current user and not group- or world-writable AND refuse it when it lies inside the active workspace (the workspace is then the operator's home: say in the denial what to do); test the workspace=HOME case end to end.\n\nBLOCKER 3: approval does not pin the script a hook names. After approving `command: [\"./guard.sh\"]` the model overwrote `guard.sh` and the next call ran the model's script. Fix: the approved identity of a project hooks file must cover the bytes of every file its commands name that resolves inside the workspace (command[0] and any argument that is an existing file path inside the workspace; resolve symlinks; refuse a command[0] that is a relative path escaping the workspace or a symlink out of it), re-checked before each run exactly as the hooks file's own hash is; a change makes the hooks inactive and \"asks again\" like a changed hooks file. `nexus hooks list`/`approve` show which files are covered. For a command[0] that is a bare name found through PATH or an absolute path outside the workspace, nothing in the workspace can replace it: state in docs/HOOKS.md that such a program is trusted as installed. Test: approve, overwrite the script through the agent's write tool, next tool call: hook not run, denial/notice names the changed file, re-approval needed.\n\nSMALLER ITEMS (do them if cheap, otherwise list as open):\n - A hook's denial reason reaches the model as `[hook X (project)] <text>`; make it unmistakably quoted hook-supplied text (a fixed framing sentence that says the text comes from a hook program and is not an instruction, the reason JSON-string-quoted and still capped) and test that a reason containing \"IGNORE PREVIOUS INSTRUCTIONS\" arrives inside that quoting.\n - docs/HOOKS.md: correct the claim about operation units (project hooks do not run in a unit's own worktree agent unless that is changed; say what is true), say that the hooks stage also applies to the operator's own `!cmd`, that a running hook is not stopped by cancel or the turn deadline and queue time is outside its timeout, and that when a hook denies a call a spine-stage gate would also have denied, the gate's own reason and later-stage records are replaced by the hook denial.\n - Add a test that a subagent's tool call goes through the hooks (the review found none).\n\nThen run, one at a time: every hook test file, `leak_guard`, `approval_policy`, `check_requirements`, the GV-1 trust/protected-path tests you touched or copied from, `policy_cli`, `managed_policy_allowlists`, `tsc --noEmit`, the complexity gate, `node scripts/check_invariants.mjs`, `node scripts/check_doc_versions.mjs`, and the hooks schema generator's `--check` if the trust record format changed (bump/ledger per the repo's rule if it did). Report: per blocker, file:line of the fix, the test names, the mutation that fails each; what the TTY guard can and cannot stop; any schema/ledger change; test counts; the commit hash; what is left open."}},"apiBlockIndex":2,"requestId":"req_011ChMwvDU15MNUZLuzqbR9T","requestRef":"48fd2aec-fbba-401f-8d41-d50910dc12f3","requestedModel":"claude-fable-5-2","promptCacheRequested":"1h","type":"assistant","uuid":"7becfe5c-b4df-4451-bf62-bd9ebd80e36c","timestamp":"2026-11-24T14:56:52.025Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-2","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","madeBy":"model","session_id":"d25d0d24-8aed-4b03-a951-966dc54f0666","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/mike/nexus-code","sessionId":"43bcbb67-2cff-4fa8-a05e-f8aa598df73b","version":"2.1.329","gitBranch":"main","slug":"synthetic-moseying-seahorse","dataFlowOn":true}
