{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-11-24T14:59:43.410Z","sessionId":"43bcbb67-2cff-4fa8-a05e-f8aa598df73b","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a51e8a083eab51d80</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>toolu_01WqpxMRfMwwtctq4h4s9TTJ</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>/tmp/claude-1000/-home-mike-nexus-code/d25d0d24-8aed-4b03-a951-966dc54f0666/tasks/a51e8a083eab51d80.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Review PR #779 PF-4 library entry\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>**Verdict: REQUEST CHANGES** at `a299f8093a827450b3a4a4d642a557e94a2bcd5b`. At the end of the review the PR was still OPEN, with the same head and not merged. The library itself is sound. The one blocker is the verifier approval in the example and docs.\n\n**Exports** (matching `docs/protocol/LIB_API_VERSIONS.json`):\n- Runtime: `NEXUS_LIB_API_VERSION`, `spawnSidecar`, `SidecarClient`, `SidecarClientError`.\n- Types: `ApprovalDecision`, `ApprovalHandler`, `ApprovalRequest`, `PromptOptions`, `SidecarChild`, `SidecarClientErrorCode`, `SidecarExit`, `SpawnSidecarOptions`, `ErrorCode`, `SidecarCommand`, `SidecarEvent`, `EngineeringOutcome`, `ExecutionState`, `TurnResult`.\n- None of these runs a turn in-process, skips managed policy or the workspace-trust check, selects auto, or grants allow-always.\n\n**Findings**\n\n1. **Blocking, by running and reading.** The example approves any request whose tool is `\"verifier\"`.\n   - Sites: `examples/embed/embed.mjs:34-38`, `docs/EMBEDDING.md` snippet, `examples/embed/README.md`. Commit a299f8 added it. The plan said the example approves only `run_command`, `write_file` and `edit_file`.\n   - A verifier approval request is raised for the configured authoritative verifier. I observed `review:{command:\"node --test\",detail:\"configured\"}`.\n   - The same tool name is used for model-declared requirement verifiers (`completion_oracle.ts:274-275`), discovered project verifiers and the type-check oracle (`completion_oracle.ts:126`). The example approves all of them without looking at the command or the origin.\n   - A library default is not at fault, because the library denies by default. But a copied example lets a project-supplied or model-declared verifier run with no human grant. The CLI asks the operator for these, and auto-approves them only with `-y`.\n   - Fix: approve only when `request.tool===\"verifier\" &amp;&amp; request.review?.command === \"node --test\" &amp;&amp; request.review?.detail === \"configured\"`, and deny the rest. Apply the same change to the docs snippet. Add a test where a model-declared verifier request is denied.\n\n2. **Non-blocking, by running.** `extraArgs: [\"-y\"]` and `[\"--mode\",\"auto\"]` reach auto approval. Both runs ended VERIFIED with zero approval requests. The sidecar does not refuse them. This is documented in `docs/EMBEDDING.md` and was accepted in plan line 310. A test that pins it would help. Also consider rejecting `-y`, `--yes` and `--mode auto` in `extraArgs`, or refusing them in the sidecar as `nexus acp` does.\n\n3. **Non-blocking, by reading.** There is no timeout on `onApproval` (`sidecar_client.ts:315`). A hung handler holds the turn until the abort signal fires. Add an optional `approvalTimeoutMs` that denies.\n\n4. **Non-blocking, by reading.** There is a test gap. The no-handler default (`sidecar_client.ts:313`) was only caught once I mutated the real decision path (see mutation M1b below). The first mutation was an equivalent mutant.\n\n5. **Non-blocking, by reading.** `tsconfig.lib.json` emits about 60 `.d.ts` files into `dist/types`, pulled in transitively. `package-linux.sh:64` copies the whole `dist` into the tarball, so the tarball grows. The `.d.ts` output is deterministic, so the two-build byte comparison should still hold. I did not run the package build.\n\n**Question 1: import side effects.** `lib.ts` imports `sidecar_client.ts`, which imports only `acp/jsonrpc.ts` and `acp/types.ts` (type imports otherwise). Tracing the graph with a resolve hook shows nothing from `node_modules` and nothing from `index.ts` (`lib_entry.test.ts:98-121`). I imported `dist/lib.js` with a nonexistent HOME: exit 0, empty output, nothing created.\n\n**Question 2: spawn.** The client runs `process.execPath` on `sidecar.js` (or `.ts` via tsx) with `-w` and the optional flags. The environment is the embedder's whole `process.env` by default, which is what the CLI does. The sidecar loads managed policy through `buildConfig` → `managedPolicy()`. It does not call `requireManagedPolicyAtStartup`. A bad policy therefore ends in a start-up failure with exit 1 rather than exit 2, which is non-blocking.\n\n**Question 3: approvals.** I exercised the client with a fake child:\n- With no handler, `{approve:true}`, `{always:true}`, `true`, `\"Approve\"` and `\"approve-always\"` all produce `deny`.\n- Only `\"approve\"` produces `approve`, always with `always:false`. A promise resolving to `\"approve\"` also works.\n- Concurrent requests are answered separately.\n- A stale approval id gets `NO_PENDING_APPROVAL` from the sidecar, which the client ignores.\n- `checkpoint.choice.request` cancels the turn.\n\n**Question 4: robustness.** Lines are bounded at 16 MiB by `BoundedLineReader`. A wrong protocol version is dropped. There is a hello timeout with a SIGKILL to the child's own pid, and `shutdown` goes SIGTERM then SIGKILL on the pid only, with no process group. `leak_guard` passes.\n\n**Question 5: packaging.** `package.json` adds `private`, `main`, `types`, `exports` and `prepublishOnly`, changes the `build` script, and keeps `bin` and the `test` scripts (`release_workflow_parity` passes). There is no lockfile change and no new dependency. `npm run build` succeeds. `dist/lib.js` and `dist/types/lib.d.ts` are produced. The smoke-package addition imports the packaged `lib.js` with no output and checks that the `.d.ts` and `sidecar.js` exist.\n\n**Question 6: acceptance.** The example spawns the real `dist/sidecar.js` against the scripted gateway. The configured verifier `node --test` is approved through the `\"verifier\"` request, which is the finding 1 path. The result is VERIFIED. The tests assert exactly `outcome: VERIFIED\\n`. A false completion claim is not VERIFIED, and a denied verifier gave UNVERIFIED in my run.\n\n**Mutations**\n\n| Mutation | Result |\n|---|---|\n| M1a: change the default initial value to approve | Equivalent mutant, no failure (overwritten by the handler result) |\n| M1b: also approve when the decision is `undefined` | Caught (2 failures) |\n| M2: let `{always:true}` through | Caught (1 failure) |\n| M3: any truthy decision allows | Caught (1 failure) |\n| M4: `lib.ts` imports `index.js` | Caught, but crudely: the test file aborts with 0 tests run, because commander runs in the runner |\n\nAll files were restored from copies.\n\n**Tests and gates**\n- Test counts: `lib_entry` 6/6, `sidecar_client` 19/19, `embed_example` 3/3, `leak_guard` 6/6, `release_workflow_parity` 6/6, `check_requirements` 28/28, `sidecar` 65/65, `protocol_parity` 5/5.\n- `tsc --noEmit` has no errors. `check_invariants`, `check_deprecated_lock` and `check_critical_complexity` all pass.\n\n**Docs (question 9).** `docs/EMBEDDING.md` is accurate, apart from the verifier snippet in finding 1. It states the limits clearly: not published, the sidecar is the trust boundary, and `extraArgs` is the embedder's own authority.\n\n**Not checked:** the full `package-linux.sh` build (twice, byte for byte), the SBOM and notices output, Node 22 versus 24, a base-branch comparison, and the sidecar hang and huge-line paths against a real child.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>86513</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>33</tool_uses><duration_ms>907192</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
