{"parentUuid":"dc77e8fa-5e6f-4505-a4ad-27dcd909101d","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-fable-5-2","id":"msg_011ChMy7vT1LPX2ZGoAMHpUv","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The hooks fixes are pushed to #782 (`258f72ff`); sending them for an independent re-review now."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1250,"cache_read_input_tokens":141929,"output_tokens":2299,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1250,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2299,"cache_read_input_tokens":141929,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1250,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1250},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011ChMy7vGbjQTYZdT56gfXW","requestRef":"087af45c-0767-4e7c-9bb4-e95966b748e4","requestedModel":"claude-fable-5-2","promptCacheRequested":"1h","type":"assistant","uuid":"1314a0a0-e1ad-4a0e-96ef-2b8f3d6520d9","timestamp":"2026-11-24T15:12:10.857Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-2","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","madeBy":"model","session_id":"d25d0d24-8aed-4b03-a951-966dc54f0666","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/mike/nexus-code","sessionId":"43bcbb67-2cff-4fa8-a05e-f8aa598df73b","version":"2.1.329","gitBranch":"main","slug":"synthetic-moseying-seahorse","dataFlowOn":true}
{"parentUuid":"1314a0a0-e1ad-4a0e-96ef-2b8f3d6520d9","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-fable-5-2","id":"msg_011ChMy7vT1LPX2ZGoAMHpUv","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01VbpBgdFNkDCgwyrZFgkNaW","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"Re-review PR #782 hooks fixes","subagent_type":"reviewer","prompt":"Independent re-review of GitHub PR #782 (HK-1 command hooks) in the repo at ~/nexus-code, base `release/v1.3.0`. Take the full head SHA from `gh pr view 782 --json headRefOid` (expected to start `258f72ff`), review exactly that, and check at the end whether it moved or was merged. The first review at `034c278c` is the PR comment starting \"Independent review at `034c278c…` — REQUEST CHANGES\" (`gh pr view 782 --comments`): read it. Your scope is the fix commit `3f769c90b` (`git show 3f769c90b --stat`, then the diff) and whether the three blockers are really closed. Try to break each one by RUNNING an attack, not by reading:\n\n1. Self-approval. The model's routes: the agent's file tools (`write_file`, `edit_file`, any other writing tool, including overwrite/rename/move/patch variants and paths with `..`, symlinks, case variations, a trailing slash, or a symlinked parent) aimed at the trust store (`extension_trust.json` under the config dir) and the operator hooks file; and `run_command \"nexus hooks approve ...\"` with no terminal. Confirm each is refused and that no hook becomes active. Check the protected-path pattern `nexus/hooks.json`: does it match `~/.config/nexus/hooks.json` when the workspace is HOME or `~/.config`? Is a project's `.nexus/hooks.json` still writable (intended)? Is the terminal check on BOTH stdin and stdout, and is there any flag, env var or code path (`--yes`, `--json`, `NEXUS_*`, the `interactive` seam being reachable from options) that approves without a terminal? Known and documented limit, do not count as a blocker but verify the docs state it honestly: an UNSANDBOXED `run_command` in auto mode can fake a terminal with `script` or write the store with a shell redirect; `nexus trust` has the same limit. Report whether, under `--sandbox`, the store is unreachable.\n2. Workspace = HOME: an operator hooks file inside the active workspace must be invalid (every call denied with guidance). Try workspace = HOME, workspace = `~/.config`, workspace = a parent of HOME, and a workspace that reaches the config dir through a symlink.\n3. Script pinning: approve a project hooks file whose command is `[\"./guard.sh\"]`, `[\"bash\", \"./guard.sh\"]`, `[\"node\", \"scripts/guard.js\", \"--flag\"]`; then, through the agent's write tool, overwrite the script → the hook must not run and the notice must name the file. Also try: replacing the script with a symlink; replacing a parent directory; an argument that is a directory; a script that did not exist at approval time and is created later; a relative path escaping the workspace; `command` given as a shell string (must be refused for project hooks); a TOCTOU swap between the hash check and the spawn (read how close the check is to the spawn; say what window remains); very large files (is hashing bounded?). Documented limits: files a pinned script itself sources, and programs outside the workspace.\n4. The denial-reason framing: a reason with newlines, quotes, 5,000 characters, and \"IGNORE PREVIOUS INSTRUCTIONS\" must arrive as one capped JSON-quoted string inside the fixed framing.\n5. Regression: the trust-record format gained an optional `files` field with the version left at 1: is an older record without `files` handled safely (asks again when the hooks run workspace files; never treated as covering them)? Does the hooks schema generator `--check` pass?\n\nThen run at head, one at a time: every `cli/test/hook*.test.ts` and `hooks_*.test.ts`, `content_trust`, `strict_json`, `doctor_hooks`, `security`, `workspace_trust`, `managed_policy_enforcement`, `approval_policy`, `leak_guard`, `check_requirements`; `cd cli && npx tsc --noEmit -p .`; the complexity gate (`npx tsx scripts/check_critical_complexity.ts` in `cli/`, or however `package.json`'s lint script invokes it); `node scripts/check_invariants.mjs`. Mutate (copy the file aside, restore from the copy, never `git checkout`): remove each protected name; remove the terminal check; skip the pinned-file comparison; accept an operator file inside the workspace. Each must fail a test.\n\nRules: do NOT edit ~/nexus-code or existing worktrees. `git -C ~/nexus-code fetch origin && git -C ~/nexus-code worktree add --detach ~/nexus-wt/rev782b <full sha>`; `cp -a ~/nexus-code/cli/node_modules ~/nexus-wt/rev782b/cli/`; `mkdir -p ~/nexus-wt/rev782b/core/target/release && cp ~/nexus-wt/ls1b/core/target/release/{harness-core,nexus-tui} ~/nexus-wt/rev782b/core/target/release/`. Tests: `cd ~/nexus-wt/rev782b/cli && TMPDIR=/dev/shm HOME=$(mktemp -d) node --import tsx --test test/<name>.test.ts 2>&1 | tail -20`, foreground. Every probe uses a throwaway HOME and temp workspace; never read or write the real ~/.nexus, ~/.config/nexus or /etc/nexus. No network, no inference. Stop processes by their own pid; never `pkill -f`. No commits, pushes or GitHub comments. At the end remove probe files and `git -C ~/nexus-code worktree remove --force ~/nexus-wt/rev782b`. Shell is zsh (`bash -c` for loops); output passes through `rtk` (use `rtk proxy` if it looks filtered); a safety check may block scripts containing `rm` — use mktemp dirs and leave them.\n\nReport under 500 words: verdict (ACCEPT / ACCEPT WITH FOLLOW-UPS / REQUEST CHANGES) at the exact SHA; per blocker: closed or not, with the attack that showed it, confirmed by running or by reading; new findings with file:line, blocking or not (any route where the agent's own tools, without an unsandboxed shell faking a terminal, put a hook in force is blocking); test counts; mutation results; what you did not check."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1250,"cache_read_input_tokens":141929,"output_tokens":2299,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1250,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2299,"cache_read_input_tokens":141929,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1250,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1250},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"toolInputStreamMs":21393,"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01VbpBgdFNkDCgwyrZFgkNaW":{"description":"Re-review PR #782 hooks fixes","subagent_type":"reviewer","prompt":"Independent re-review of GitHub PR #782 (HK-1 command hooks) in the repo at ~/nexus-code, base `release/v1.3.0`. Take the full head SHA from `gh pr view 782 --json headRefOid` (expected to start `258f72ff`), review exactly that, and check at the end whether it moved or was merged. The first review at `034c278c` is the PR comment starting \"Independent review at `034c278c…` — REQUEST CHANGES\" (`gh pr view 782 --comments`): read it. Your scope is the fix commit `3f769c90b` (`git show 3f769c90b --stat`, then the diff) and whether the three blockers are really closed. Try to break each one by RUNNING an attack, not by reading:\n\n1. Self-approval. The model's routes: the agent's file tools (`write_file`, `edit_file`, any other writing tool, including overwrite/rename/move/patch variants and paths with `..`, symlinks, case variations, a trailing slash, or a symlinked parent) aimed at the trust store (`extension_trust.json` under the config dir) and the operator hooks file; and `run_command \"nexus hooks approve ...\"` with no terminal. Confirm each is refused and that no hook becomes active. Check the